#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2e55436-aec3-564a-9eb4-a00dac462569" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Residual for a Data Point"}] :question/id #uuid "b74e2c6d-183c-5130-b66b-c01c1bc5503b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5726" :question/problem "![](images/25/257982e25d43c2eb8e2fe9e8171513385bc21659a2526666e75194f7cf0f2042.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows a relationship between the concentration of a water pollutant ($x$, measured in $\\mathrm{mg/mL}$) and the number of animal species $(y)$ that live in some ponds in a particular region. The corresponding quadratic regression curve is also shown.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted data point?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the regression model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Residual} = \\text{Actual} - \\textrm{Estimated}\n$$\n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(80,60)$. So, our actual value is $y=60$.\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression curve that corresponds to $x=80$ has the $y$-coordinate $50$. So, our estimated value is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{y}=50\n$$\n\n![](images/49/49014ffe0943d7ce18084c359bc09ab0877cbc93a7d5df4beff83b0dadce47b7.png)\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nResidual &= Actual - Estimated \\\\\n&= y - y^(^) \\\\\n&= 60 - 50 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $10$ animal species."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "782a6a0a-7a15-5230-8f9d-5405aa72f96a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Measure of a Combined Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "150dcf2e-cf5d-580c-80cf-4de132de6ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{114}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432378} {:answer/id #uuid "5c635d44-f527-52b7-8ae0-be4f7a219e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{100}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432402} {:answer/id #uuid "820cb5ee-19b1-5f2b-9761-e32669a23448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{110}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432414} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cea861-4883-5b3d-9ff5-0bc5b21f4817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{102}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432434} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd5e54d-ea4c-5c63-8f8e-9e59e4fa7ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{105}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "820cb5ee-19b1-5f2b-9761-e32669a23448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{110}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432414} :answer-field/id #uuid "98bae0c0-0e36-5a95-ba86-416da6d6e052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15477a81-cabf-5246-8bf7-8ff2a8a5aa83" :question/math-academy-id "q-21727" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd99171c2696038851bf0fdf7297d2e4a60791f6b11083af8cd51baf48930b2d.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overset{^}{COE}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/67952e3c3d8b030327cbae686da23d466d509de9171242f5ccc4e502f10813d7.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\overset{^}{COD}$ and $\\overset{^}{DOE}$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overset{\\to }{OD}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\overset{^}{COE} & =m\\overset{^}{COD}+m\\overset{^}{DOE} \\\\  & ={63}^{∘}+{47}^{∘} \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133909 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "816d35ed-007d-58b9-9c59-16302359e658" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Angle With a Given Measure"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3a7807-68d4-502b-8923-21835233871d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{BOD}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430832} {:answer/id #uuid "9d820a32-8170-596e-b87b-56276a347c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{AOB}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430867} {:answer/id #uuid "bffa61d0-5b92-5b7d-bf33-cde6411276d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{COD}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430874} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e3c463-8f1b-5a34-98c5-3d7fab0ad4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{AOD}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430879} {:answer/id #uuid "f68e9b0d-3b3d-5a3a-9660-30fdf36552cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{BOC}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bffa61d0-5b92-5b7d-bf33-cde6411276d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{COD}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430874} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d91f790-cdad-5b43-976a-ae7e27bf9baa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2107a33-4f8b-533f-ae6c-505eca3a5d96" :question/math-academy-id "q-150809" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd5841753edf7b3d2b0008057777e0b0338f54d3188c398bbc85d9c897d6ea64.png)\n\nWhich angle has a measure of ${30}^{∘}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the angle with a measure ${30}^{∘}$ on our diagram.\n\n![](images/54/547ec18ef591f22b438fd6713ed7b5e67aaab645a9379312c9b5149b0c290ff3.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the angle $\\overset{^}{COD}$ (or $\\overset{^}{DOC}$) has a measure of ${30}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7eff006-e868-5994-a895-708ff74808b4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Angles Using Angle Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b87eb4e2-bb06-5914-a735-1bb12e95d9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131085} {:answer/id #uuid "ccad4e2a-5678-540f-83c3-824f846d9c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131086} {:answer/id #uuid "0648e602-723e-5ff9-92c4-4b6e04a8149b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131087} {:answer/id #uuid "5965c6c8-cf10-5f8a-b323-d4e38efcf209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131088} {:answer/id #uuid "63896d06-0613-52d7-acd4-180e4535a578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∠2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131089}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9fa1abdd-5f09-57fc-92a4-206ec0661be8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131084}] :question/id #uuid "065bc3f1-8232-5f47-a810-b6324457643f" :question/math-academy-id "q-21724" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c35ebfcad87dca92f765ef586729356a45266292c10e17628a8b2efd0541c06.png)\n\nWhich numbered angle refers to the same angle as $FGD^(^)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133910 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "773c6135-d786-593d-8fa6-92e929904554" :knowledge-point/title "Reading the Measure of an Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5ce66a0-3707-59b8-953d-d177e8bfc571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131103} {:answer/id #uuid "a79fa280-6482-5be5-bd4e-7ab918d50602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131104} {:answer/id #uuid "defe29ca-c619-5935-a846-3a2d8678bace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "140^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131105} {:answer/id #uuid "11da40a0-5413-59b2-bf90-733958036169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131106} {:answer/id #uuid "1b84180e-70e8-5e19-8db4-4edf476e2e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131107}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c0f3227e-e328-547e-b9fe-b859faa86831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131102}] :question/id #uuid "cb73d2ee-0ea2-5bd1-b88e-26cdd09b8bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-21873" :question/problem "![](images/8e/8edd3796a19599d80703874d8ef4631b4c0c5100f74f6bd4a4d2c138883a3123.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of the angle $CGE^(^)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157172 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "a485378b-0bce-5576-bcbc-2ef77df2da17" :question/math-academy-id "e-7882" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n6, \\quad 13, \\quad 7, \\quad 5, \\quad 14 , \\quad 11, \\quad 7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu.$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(6+13+7+5+14+11+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{7} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161703 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/id #uuid "1ad81fed-5e71-5df6-abc4-a09cd5c432fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10362" :question/problem "Suppose that in a population of children with only one pet, $42\\%$ of children have a dog. A random sample of $n = 40$ children is taken from the population and asked what pet they have.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of children in the sample that have a dog? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n![](images/41/412ed703d6c44b1e664317fd734871cfe71d14c944cfa9f9bca84752f635836e.svg)\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}  = \\overline{X} = \\dfrac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p = 42 \\% = 0.42,$ and $n = 40.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =42%=0.42, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{40} \\\\  & ≈0.0061\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161737 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "476d910b-b0b7-545c-8bf9-b8b3d6c68fb0" :knowledge-point/title "Carrying Out a Chi-Square Test (Regrouping Needed)"}] :question/id #uuid "2bc2b059-777b-5da0-ad59-fb6505c0de37" :question/math-academy-id "e-16477" :question/problem "The manager of a chocolate shop believes that the weekly number of customer complaints about the service follows a Poisson distribution with rate parameter $\\lambda$. The frequency distribution of the complaints over $40$ weeks is summarized below.\n\n| Number of Complaints $(y_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\geq3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequencies $(O_i)$ | $20$ | $12$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nConduct a chi-square goodness of fit test at the $10\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n- $H_1:\\: Y\\not\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n\nwhere the rate parameter $\\lambda$ is unknown.\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = p$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\,6.635\\,$ | $\\,3.841\\,$ | $\\,2.706\\,$ |\n| $2$ | $\\,9.210\\,$ | $\\,5.991\\,$ | $\\,4.605\\,$ |\n| $3$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nLet $Y$ be the number of customer complaints received per week. Under the null hypothesis, we have that $Y \\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN = 20+12+8 =40\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimator for $\\lambda$, which we denote as $\\hat \\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^(^) &= \\frac{total number of events}{total number of observations} \\\\\n&= (∑(y_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $O_i$ is the observed frequency for the $i$th observation, $N$ is the number of observations. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^(^) &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 20 + 1 \\cdot 12 + 2 \\cdot 8}{40} \\\\\n&= 0.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(0.7)$.\n\nUnder the hypothesis that $Y \\sim \\mathrm{Po}(0.7)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y = y) &= \\frac{λ^{y}e^{- λ}}{y!} \\\\\n&= ((0.7)^{y}e^{-0.7})/(y!), y = 0, 1, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y = 0) &= ((0.7)^{0}e^{-0.7})/(0!) \\approx 0.496585 \\\\\nP(Y = 1) &= ((0.7)^{1}e^{-0.7})/(1!) \\approx 0.347610 \\\\\nP(Y = 2) &= ((0.7)^{2}e^{-0.7})/(2!) \\approx 0.121663 \\\\\nP(Y \\ge 3) &= 1 - P(Y = 0) - P(Y = 1) - P(Y = 2) = 0.034142\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 0) = 40 \\cdot 0.496585 \\approx 19.8634 \\\\\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 1) = 40 \\cdot 0.347610 \\approx 13.9044 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 2) = 40 \\cdot 0.121663 \\approx 4.8665 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(Y \\ge 3) = 40 \\cdot 0.034142 \\approx 1.3657\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that there are two frequencies less than $5$, so we combine the values $Y = 2$ and $Y=3$.\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding theoretical frequencies is given below:\n\n| $y_i$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\geq2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $20$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n| $E_i$ | $19.8634$ | $13.9044$ | $6.2322$ |\n| $\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$ | $0.0009$ | $0.2608$ | $0.5014$ |\n\nWe have rounded values to four decimal places, where appropriate.\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 0.0009 + 0.2608 + 0.5014 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.8}$ (rounded to one decimal place).\n\nOur data has two constraints: the total number of observations must equal $N$, and the estimated rate $\\hat \\lambda$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 3 - 2 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** We must use the number of categories in the combined table!\n\nSo, there is\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\ndegree of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu = 1$ at a $10\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}} = 2.706\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 2.706\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/15/155070611813bc121d3af67bb5e53c5504bfa3e089a4b0b1d491e8f351f36bc7.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(0.8)$ is not inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161738 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98b78bea-df1e-56c8-9f6e-d35993136c76" :knowledge-point/title "Carrying Out a Chi-Square Test (Parameter Estimation Needed)"}] :question/id #uuid "8a0f9805-00a6-5bf4-9ef8-5678f99d418a" :question/math-academy-id "e-16502" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a continuous random variable. We wish to conduct a chi-square goodness-of-fit test at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ $Y\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ $Y\\not\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$\n\nwhere the mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$ are unknown.\n\nA random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observations $(y_i)$ | Frequency $(O_i)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $1\\leq y_i < 5$ | $11$ |\n| $5\\leq y_i < 9$ | $12$ |\n| $9\\leq y_i < 13$ | $25$ |\n| $13\\leq y_i < 17$ | $19$ |\n| $17\\leq y_i < 21$ | $13$ |\n\nSome summary statistics for this data set are given below. Each expected frequency $E_i$ is calculated assuming the null hypothesis is true using the estimates $\\widehat{\\mu} \\approx 11.6$ and $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2\\approx 25.4$.\n\n| $y_i$ | $O_i$ | $E_i$ | $\\dfrac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1\\leq y_i < 5$ | $11$ | $6.2992$ | $3.5080$ |\n| $5\\leq y_i < 9$ | $12$ | $16.7652$ | $1.3544$ |\n| $9\\leq y_i < 13$ | $25$ | $24.5351$ | $0.0088$ |\n| $13\\leq y_i < 17$ | $19$ | $19.7586$ | $0.0291$ |\n| $17\\leq y_i < 21$ | $13$ | $8.7523$ | $2.0615$ |\n\nCarry out this hypothesis test and state your conclusion." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nFirst, note that the number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 11 + 12 + 25 + 19 + 13 = 80\n$$\n\nWe first need to find estimates for the mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$. We start by finding the midpoint of each observational class.\n\n| Observations $(y_i)$ | Frequency $(O_i)$ | $y_i^{\\textrm{mid}}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $1\\leq y_i < 5$ | $11$ | $3$ |\n| $5\\leq y_i < 9$ | $12$ | $7$ |\n| $9\\leq y_i < 13$ | $25$ | $11$ |\n| $13\\leq y_i < 17$ | $19$ | $15$ |\n| $17\\leq y_i < 21$ | $13$ | $19$ |\n\nWe can find an estimate $\\widehat{\\mu}$ for the mean $\\mu$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ^(^) &= \\frac{1}{N}∑O_{i} \\cdot y_{i}^{mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{80}(11 \\cdot 3 + 12 \\cdot 7 + 25 \\cdot 11 + 19 \\cdot 15 + 13 \\cdot 19) \\\\\n&= 11.55 \\\\\n&\\approx 11.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find an estimate $\\widehat{\\sigma}^2$ for the variance $\\sigma^2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ^(^)^{2} &= \\frac{1}{N - 1}∑O_{i} \\cdot (y_{i}^{mid} - μ^(^))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{80 - 1}(11 \\cdot (3 - 11.55)^{2} + 12 \\cdot (7 - 11.55)^{2} \\\\\n&= + 25 \\cdot (11 - 11.55)^{2} + 19 \\cdot (15 - 11.55)^{2} + 13 \\cdot (19 - 11.55)^{2}) \\\\\n&= 25.4151… \\\\\n&\\approx 25.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur data has three constraints: the total number of observations must equal $N$, and our estimates for the mean and variance are $11.6$ and $25.4$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 5 - 3 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nWe compute the chi-square test statistic as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 3.5080 + 1.3544 + 0.0088 + 0.0291 + 2.0615 \\\\\n&\\approx 7.0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7.0}$.\n\nNotice that the test statistic does not lie inside the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d955393fa1665ae7d971eb104a009e2038484f19f3c29a5134a01854deba69c0.png)\n\nTherefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28864a7b-1069-5064-88d9-de4fce2ee733" :knowledge-point/title "Carrying Out a Chi-Square Test"}] :question/id #uuid "ea87bc19-0bbf-5b4e-a59a-b576c1637c43" :question/math-academy-id "e-9322" :question/problem "A basketball player practices free throws, shooting $5$ times per session and tracking the number of successful shots. The frequency distribution of successful shots per session is summarized below.\n\n| Successful Shots $(y_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequencies $(O_i)$ | $12$ | $32$ | $67$ | $52$ | $27$ | $10$ |\n\nThe player's coach wants to test whether a binomial distribution can model the observed data.\nCarry out a chi-square goodness-of-fit test at the $10\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The observed distribution can be modeled as $B(5, p)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The observed distribution cannot be modeled as $B(5, p)$\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = q$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus q$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,13.277\\,$ | $\\,9.488\\,$ | $\\,7.779\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,15.086\\,$ | $\\,11.070\\,$ | $\\,9.236\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 12+32+67+52+27+10 = 200\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimate of $p$, which we denote as $\\hat p$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^) &= \\frac{total number of successes}{total number of trials} \\\\\n&= (∑(y_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(n \\cdot N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the number of observations, and $n$ is the number of trials in each binomial sequence. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^) &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 12 + 1 \\cdot 32 + 2 \\cdot 67 + 3 \\cdot 52 + 4 \\cdot 27 + 5 \\cdot 0}{5 \\cdot 200} \\\\\n&= 0.48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $Y\\sim B(5, \\, 0.48)$.\n\nUnder the hypothesis that $Y\\sim B(5, \\, 0.48)$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( Y = y ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{y} \\right) {p}^{y} ( 1 - p {)}^{n - y} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{y} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{y} ( 0.52 {)}^{5 - y} , \\hspace{2em} y = 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( Y = 0 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{0} ( 0.52 {)}^{5} = 0.038 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 1 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{1} ( 0.52 {)}^{4} = 0.1755 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 2 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{2} ( 0.52 {)}^{3} = 0.324 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{3} ( 0.52 {)}^{2} = 0.299 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 4 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{4} ( 0.52 {)}^{1} = 0.138 \\\\ \\displaystyle P ( Y = 5 ) & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5} \\right) ( 0.48 {)}^{5} ( 0.52 {)}^{0} = 0.0255\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 0) = 200 \\cdot 0.0380 = 7.6 \\\\\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 1) = 200 \\cdot 0.1755 = 35.1 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 2) = 200 \\cdot 0.3240 = 64.8 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 3) = 200 \\cdot 0.2990 = 59.8 \\\\\nE_{4} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 4) = 200 \\cdot 0.1380 = 27.6 \\\\\nE_{5} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 5) = 200 \\cdot 0.0255 = 5.1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding theoretical frequencies is given below:\n\n| $y_i$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $12$ | $32$ | $67$ | $52$ | $27$ | $10$ |\n| $E_i$ | $7.6$ | $35.1$ | $64.8$ | $59.8$ | $27.6$ | $5.1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$ | $2.5474$ | $0.2738$ | $0.0747$ | $1.0174$ | $0.0130$ | $4.7078$ |\n\nWe have rounded values to four decimal places, where appropriate. Notice that all of the expected frequencies are greater than or equal to $5$.\n\nFirst, we compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 2.5474 + 0.2738 + 0.0747 + 1.0173 + 0.0130 + 4.7078 \\\\\n&\\approx 8.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8.6}$.\n\nThere are two constraints on our data: the total number of observations must equal $N$, and our estimated probability of success $\\hat p$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 6 - 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=4$ at a $10\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=7.779\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 7.779\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/39/3949c36fa794d40d277708c4ac5808c5895fa40c327445f1fbeb99af7bd136e2.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(8.6)$ lies inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161946 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "396ce5d1-5c31-5cc2-a2d6-95ae3ed3800a" :knowledge-point/title "Carrying Out a Chi-Square Test"}] :question/id #uuid "e37f9c03-ca4e-51e8-b862-dcf4f94d758a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9326" :question/problem "A shopping center manager believes that the average number of customer complaints received on a randomly selected day follows a Poisson distribution $\\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$. The frequency distribution of the complaints received over a period of $50$ days is summarized below.\n\n| Number of Complaints ($y_i$) | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\geq 4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Frequency ($O_i$) | $6$ | $12$ | $18$ | $14$ | $0$ |\n\nConduct a chi-square goodness of fit test at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The observed distribution can be modeled as $\\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The observed distribution cannot be modeled as $\\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = p$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\,6.635\\,$ | $\\,3.841\\,$ | $\\,2.706\\,$ |\n| $2$ | $\\,9.210\\,$ | $\\,5.991\\,$ | $\\,4.605\\,$ |\n| $3$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a set of observed frequencies $O_i$ and corresponding expected frequencies $E_i$, as defined by a theoretical model under the null hypothesis. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nY= \\sum_{i} \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_i$ is the observed frequency for category $i$, and\n- $E_i$ is the expected frequency for category $i$, as predicted under the null hypothesis.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_i$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_i \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each category), and the observations are independent.\n\nLet the random variable $Y$ be the number of complaints received on a randomly selected day.\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN= 6 + 12 + 18 + 14= 50\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimate for $\\lambda$, which we denote as $\\hat \\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^(^) &= \\frac{total number of events}{total number of observations} \\\\\n&= (∑(y_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $O_i$ is the observed frequency for the $i$th observation, and $N$ is the number of observations. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^(^) &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 6 + 1 \\cdot 12 + 2 \\cdot 18 + 3 \\cdot 14}{50} \\\\\n&= 1.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(1.8)$.\n\nUnder the hypothesis that $Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(1.8)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y = y) &= \\frac{λ^{y}e^{- λ}}{y!} \\\\\n&= ((1.8)^{y}e^{-1.8})/(y!), y = 0, 1, 2, 3, …\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the probability of each value of $Y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y = 0) &= ((1.8)^{0}e^{-1.8})/(0!) \\approx 0.165299 \\\\\nP(Y = 1) &= ((1.8)^{1}e^{-1.8})/(1!) \\approx 0.297538 \\\\\nP(Y = 2) &= ((1.8)^{2}e^{-1.8})/(2!) \\approx 0.267784 \\\\\nP(Y = 3) &= ((1.8)^{3}e^{-1.8})/(3!) \\approx 0.160671 \\\\\nP(Y \\ge 4) &= 1 - P(Y = 0) - P(Y = 1) - P(Y = 2) - P(Y = 3) = 0.108708\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each observation are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{0} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 0) = 50 \\cdot 0.165299 \\approx 8.2650 \\\\\nE_{1} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 1) = 50 \\cdot 0.297538 \\approx 14.8769 \\\\\nE_{2} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 2) = 50 \\cdot 0.267784 \\approx 13.3892 \\\\\nE_{3} &= N \\cdot P(Y = 3) = 50 \\cdot 0.160671 \\approx 8.0336 \\\\\nE_{4} &= N \\cdot P(Y \\ge 4) = 50 \\cdot 0.108708 \\approx 5.4354\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding theoretical frequencies is given below:\n\n| $y_i$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\geq 4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $O_i$ | $6$ | $12$ | $18$ | $14$ | $0$ |\n| $E_i$ | $8.2650$ | $14.8769$ | $13.3892$ | $8.0336$ | $5.4354$ |\n| $\\dfrac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}$ | $0.6207$ | $0.5563$ | $1.5878$ | $4.4311$ | $5.4354$ |\n\nNotice that the expected frequencies are greater than or equal to $5$. Also, we have rounded values to four decimal places, where appropriate.\n\nWe compute our test statistic by summing the last row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∑_(i)((O_{i} - E_{i})^{2})/(E_{i}) &= 0.6207 + 0.5563 + 1.5878 + 4.4311 + 5.4354 \\\\\n&= 12.6313\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}12.6}$ (rounded to one decimal place).\n\nOur data has two constraints: the total number of observations must equal $N$, and the estimated rate parameter $\\hat \\lambda$. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of categories - number of constraints \\\\\n&= 5 - 2 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, there are\n\n$$\n\\nu = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=3$ at a $1\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=11.345\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nY\\geq 11.345\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3f28000117b2d51899700d0e995c44e556f15be3d740cbac98e4e1a3c95ff709.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(12.6)$ lies inside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161890 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8c299c1-12b2-5a1f-b4a2-ee298ac8485e" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Rate Parameter From Sample Data"}] :question/id #uuid "fd33baa3-d81c-5cc2-8073-6d5255d0da04" :question/math-academy-id "e-9341" :question/problem "Suppose $Y$ is a random variable with support $S=\\{0,1,2,3, \\ldots\\}$. A random sample is carried out, and the sample data is shown below.\n\n| Observation $(y_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\geq 4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Observed Frequency $(O_i)$ | $30$ | $27$ | $10$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness of fit test is conducted at the $10\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n- $H_1:\\: Y\\not\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\lambda)$\n\nUse this data to calculate an estimator for the rate $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "The number of observations is\n\n$$\nN=30+27+10+3=70\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimator for $\\lambda$, which we denote as $\\hat \\lambda$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^(^) &= \\frac{total number of events}{total number of observations} \\\\\n&= (∑(y_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $O_i$ is the observed frequency for the $i$th observation, $N$ is the number of observations. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^(^) &= \\frac{0 \\cdot 30 + 1 \\cdot 27 + 2 \\cdot 10 + 3 \\cdot 3}{70} \\\\\n&= 0.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $Y\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(0.8)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161893 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161941 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e72cf3d1-ca9b-5995-aa85-dc540208ca66" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Binomial Probability From Sample Data"}] :question/id #uuid "554461a5-52a5-5c44-b3ce-b1bb42b92cee" :question/math-academy-id "e-9353" :question/problem "A food quality control team tests boxes of packaged salads to check for damage. Each box contains $3$ salad packs. The results of the test are summarized below.\n\n| Number of Damaged Packs in a Box $(x_i)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Frequency $(O_i)$ | $20$ | $30$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n\nA chi-square goodness-of-fit test is conducted at the $1\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\:$ The sample data fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n- $H_1:\\:$ The sample data does not fit a binomial distribution $B(3,p)$\n\nUse this data to calculate an estimate of $p$." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of damaged packs in a randomly selected box.\n\nFirst, we write down the null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:\\: X\\sim B(3,p)$\n- $H_1:\\:X\\not\\sim B(3,p)$\n\nThe number of observations is\n\n$$\nN=20+30+8+2 = 60\n$$\n\nWe can calculate an estimate of $p$, which we denote as $\\hat p$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^) &= \\frac{total number of successes}{total number of trials} \\\\\n&= (∑(x_{i} \\cdot O_{i}))/(n \\cdot N)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $O_i$ is the observed frequency for the $i$th observation, $N$ is the number of observations, and $n$ is the number of trials in each Binomial sequence. So, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^) &= (0 \\cdot 20 + 1 \\cdot 30 + 2 \\cdot 8 + 3 \\cdot 2)/(3 \\cdot (60)) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.2889\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the proposed distribution is $X\\sim B(3,0.2889)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162115 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62aa30b-6bf5-5c06-bb1f-8269a6806368" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Discrete Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98fd53c6-1ce1-5e29-9342-f54498e4657c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159573} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b9c065-0650-5950-b74e-3025af0b5186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159574} {:answer/id #uuid "0b801eae-251d-5746-a091-62c984a643d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159575} {:answer/id #uuid "24d3dac4-c1cf-5041-9181-26b68f19294c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159576} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9dd77b-61c8-547a-ae1d-23baa0d22812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1b9c065-0650-5950-b74e-3025af0b5186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159574} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2a9a069-b73f-5a6f-bd50-90972ad14ac2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04a3c966-c6bc-5da3-b178-0b866e10e919" :question/math-academy-id "q-143525" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ),\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }+\\frac{1}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }+\\frac{1}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\theta }+\\frac{1}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5(\\theta -1)+\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 5(\\theta -1)+\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 6\\theta -5 & =0 \\\\ 6\\theta  & =5 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{5}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0896db64-9d17-550f-8644-691706316afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.45,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0175" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441031} {:answer/id #uuid "174dc03d-1058-5222-a2c8-328de8d3dfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.45,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441036} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea9fa72-6c20-5814-8a9d-b2946f694c67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.45,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441042} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee15f42-cfc1-5622-b98b-eb798bf29abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.55,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0231" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441049} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7e5957-681c-5abe-8f63-81da11f56105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.55,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "174dc03d-1058-5222-a2c8-328de8d3dfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.45,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441036} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff33034c-efba-5bcc-a6c7-948ce58e66bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fba7207c-8537-5b1a-b7be-0bdd1b79e13d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146295" :question/problem "Suppose that $45%$ of the voting population plans to vote for candidate $A$ in the upcoming mayoral election. A random sample of size $n=20$ is taken from the population and asked about their voting preference. \n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of voters in the sample that plan to vote for candidate $A?$ Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=45%=0.45,$ and $n=20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =45%=0.45, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{20} \\\\  & ≈0.0124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083a6222-5a52-5710-852c-3e97ebe97c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555015} {:answer/id #uuid "2c027381-40ea-5799-9ee6-5c1914e36adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555098} {:answer/id #uuid "d70aa7b2-f393-5544-ab75-58709dc4953c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.85,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0036" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555513} {:answer/id #uuid "e788456d-4b2f-5a3b-9773-97044c05216e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0036" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555568} {:answer/id #uuid "fa98449f-940e-591b-be82-68f3da8ba744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.85,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "083a6222-5a52-5710-852c-3e97ebe97c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555015} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4ebb605-8da3-5343-bd47-314118a290ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "010791ef-9757-53ad-8c05-5eece41ad7e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-146454" :question/problem "It is known that $15%$ of a particular teenage population ride bicycles for exercise. A random sample of $n=60$ teenagers is taken from the population and asked whether they ride a bicycle for exercise.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of teenagers in the sample who ride bicycles? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=15%=0.15,$ and $n=60.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =15%=0.15, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{60} \\\\  & ≈0.0021\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162115 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62aa30b-6bf5-5c06-bb1f-8269a6806368" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Discrete Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a069d3d7-1df7-5483-81b8-15c827c9fe6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159927} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c6a07d-3111-5a79-a38b-8f66ebcd4040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159928} {:answer/id #uuid "a6133dab-cb89-51c2-b335-573a1ca738a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159929} {:answer/id #uuid "17f34a8c-1fd2-59f6-b7bb-2ebe5ca8c301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159930} {:answer/id #uuid "60e73cef-e77b-57eb-89da-2b6a8daebcd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159931}] :answer-field/id #uuid "482084e5-6a55-5752-89ae-309fa78b4ac5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159926}] :question/id #uuid "0763eca4-0450-541b-8145-1b9e94f6511b" :question/math-academy-id "q-159836" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent binomial random variables is\n$l(θ) = \\ln 27 + 5\\ln θ + 7\\ln (1 - θ)$,\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $θ^(^)$ from this sample?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162116 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78241be4-1db7-554c-a7ce-7532345f3f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Continuous Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7d9e04d-364d-5cd2-a76b-079b31420a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.318" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159933} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcf9af6-834e-5e62-9b4a-13805ba8982b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.405" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159934} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8f1126-0cf1-58d5-bae9-e75683d66a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159935} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbf1f59-a534-529c-86f1-597a8eeda6c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.282" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159936} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6198e8-77ff-51e2-9658-4fb9e857ddc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159937}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6002c10f-c80d-5881-8f7e-5f4a293c8da1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159932}] :question/id #uuid "dd886376-5e92-5c56-bf3b-e94156a7bb4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159837" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent exponential random variables is\n$l(θ) = 8\\ln θ - 28.4θ$,\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $θ^(^)$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162116 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78241be4-1db7-554c-a7ce-7532345f3f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Continuous Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9269a9e9-1388-5531-911b-5d5ae8707e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.928" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159939} {:answer/id #uuid "4a8866c5-4ae9-5f21-9817-a05815267e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.905" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159940} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4a56ee-68b3-57e9-baee-0b3ef35b9ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.141" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159941} {:answer/id #uuid "70284d8d-13cc-5cdf-9352-4c005fc6bc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.284" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159942} {:answer/id #uuid "7b282090-a586-5823-9b53-fa5078507f22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.517" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159943}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dac01319-30c1-5506-81a8-79ad80920b3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159938}] :question/id #uuid "0ccc1b8e-d017-5c69-b564-db7386631bbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-159838" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n$l(θ) =-6\\ln (2π) - 45.3 + 30.2θ - 6θ^{2}$,\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $θ^(^)$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171760 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171970 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ef114a4-dd89-59a4-ad53-c2d107dc84dd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Sample Size When a Point Estimate Is Given"}] :question/id #uuid "7d60a41a-4357-5b32-9bf7-047492fcad03" :question/math-academy-id "e-15098" :question/problem "A company wants to estimate the proportion of customers who prefer their new product over a competing product. The company believes that approximately $84\\%$ of customers prefer their product. Find a lower bound on the sample size $n$ required to ensure that the margin of error for the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of satisfied customers is at most $0.06$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} \\leq \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = 0.025\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96\n$$\n\nWe're given the estimate\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 84\\% = 0.84\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.06, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=0.84, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=1.96\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}); = ((1.96)^{2}0.84(1 - 0.84))/((0.06)^{2}); = 143.42\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that\n\n$$\nn=\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}144}\n$$\n\nor larger."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171762 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171973 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "918f0c69-95cd-59c7-a397-d566b585a088" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Minimum Sample Size Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "ac3d869f-7681-5a12-b1f5-dd7380286de6" :question/math-academy-id "e-15101" :question/problem "Consider a population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Let $I_1$ be a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic obtained from a sample of size $n_1$.\n\nSuppose we require a $99\\%$ confidence interval $I_2$ to have approximately the same length as $I_1$. Fill in the missing information to find the sample size $n_2$ we should use to get $I_2$. You may assume that the point estimate $\\widehat p$ is the same for the two samples.\n\nYou may use the following percentage points table for the normal distribution.\n\n| $\\quad z \\quad$ | $0.95$ | $0.975$ | $0.98$ | $0.99$ | $0.995$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,1.645\\,$ | $\\,1.960\\,$ | $\\,2.054\\,$ | $\\,2.326\\,$ | $\\,2.576\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nGiven a particular margin or error $\\varepsilon$, we can deduce an approximate sample size:\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} = \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nLet $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ be the margins of error of $I_1$ and $I_2$, respectively. Since we want $I_2$ to have the same length as $I_1$, then\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon_1 = \\varepsilon_2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{1} &= (z_{0.05}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε_{1}^{2}), \\\\\nn_{2} &= (z_{0.005}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε_{2}^{2}), \\\\\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{z_{0.005}^{2}}{z_{0.05}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nz_{0.05} = 1.645\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_{0.005}=2.576\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n_2}{n_1} = \\dfrac{2.576^2}{1.645^2} \\approx 2.5\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nn_2\\approx 2.5 n_1\n$$\n\nFinally, $n_2$ must be $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{larger}}$ than $n_1$ by a factor of approximately $2.5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171763 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171972 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee69a01-1a62-52e1-9ed0-8d019b059d00" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Sample Size When an Interval Estimate Is Given"}] :question/id #uuid "4bbc07d8-6118-526c-b2a7-aae46e15a8ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-15109" :question/problem "A pizzeria estimates that the number of orders delivered within $30$ minutes is between $60\\%$ and $72\\%$. The restaurant's management decides that they want a more precise estimate.\nFind a lower bound for sample size $n$ required to ensure that the margin of error for the $98\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of the pizzeria's orders is at most $0.04$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 2.326) = 0.01$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} \\leq \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $98\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.98=0.02 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.01\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.326) = 0.01\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.01} = 2.326\n$$\n\nWe have the estimate $p \\in [0.6, 0.72]$. So, to find our lower bound for $n$, we replace $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with the maximum of the function\n\n$$\nf(p) = p(1-p)\n$$\n\nfor $p\\in[0.6, 0.72]$.\n\n![](images/31/31ff79710bc98bba503fd10accbb0118d596fc5fa4684e4c376f31f8183adcd6.png)\n\nSince $f$ decreases in the given interval, the maximum occurs at $p=0.6$. Therefore, we replace $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with\n\n$$\n0.6(1-0.6)=0.24\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.04, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})=0.24, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=2.326\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}); = ((2.326)^{2}0.24)/((0.04)^{2}); = 811.54\n$$\n\nRounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=812$ or larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\nn_{\\textrm{min}} = 812\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171766 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "79725d6e-c092-550c-8afb-7f3b57f54e92" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Sample Size Using the Empirical Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "e7288503-fea2-599a-afde-ae561f340e34" :question/math-academy-id "e-15121" :question/problem "Consider a normal population with unknown mean and variance, for which it is known that $99.7\\%$ of the observations lie between $-5.3$ and $12.7$, and this data is distributed symmetrically about the mean.\nUsing the empirical rule to estimate the population standard deviation, find the smallest sample size $n$ required to ensure that the margin of error for the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean is at most $0.5$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\overline{\\,x}$ is the sample mean,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $\\sigma$ is the population standard deviation, and\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n} \\leq \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n \\geq \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\sigma^2}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nTo find an estimate for $\\sigma^2$, we can apply the empirical rule. According to this rule, $99.7\\%$ of the observations of a random normal variable $N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ fall in the interval $(\\mu-3\\sigma, \\mu+3\\sigma)$. Therefore, we can estimate $\\sigma$ by dividing the given range by\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3 = 6{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\sigma} =\\dfrac{12.7-(-5.3)}{6} = 3\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = 0.025\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.5, \\qquad \\widehat{\\sigma}=3, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=1.96\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\nn \\mid \\ge (z_{α/2}^{2}σ^(^)^{2})/(ε^{2}); = ((1.96)^{2}(3)^{2})/((0.5)^{2}); \\approx 138.30\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=139$ or larger."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171768 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171974 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "18ae46a4-cb19-5281-8033-2934c7a61247" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Sample Size When a Point Estimate Is Given"}] :question/id #uuid "f0ce4bd7-5d35-50f7-be30-172068f660c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-15225" :question/problem "A scientist is studying a population of $630$ cats of a particular breed. They believe that $10\\%$ of the cats have a certain genetic disorder.\n\nFind a lower bound for the number of cats the scientist should examine to ensure that the margin of error for the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of the cats with the disorder is at most $0.02$.\n\n*Hint: Use a finite population correction factor. You may also use the fact that $P(Z > 2.576) = 0.005$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ for a small population can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $N$ is the population size satisfying the condition $n > 0.05\\cdot N$,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}} \\leq \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nDoing some algebra to isolate $n$, we get\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\dfrac{Nm}{N + m - 1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nis the minimum sample size needed when the population is large.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.576) = 0.005\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.005} = 2.576\n$$\n\nWe're given the estimate\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 10\\% = 0.1\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.02, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=0.1, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=2.576\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((2.576)^{2}0.1(1 - 0.1))/((0.02)^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 1493.05, \\\\\n&= n \\mid \\ge \\frac{Nm}{N + m - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{630 \\cdot 1493.05}{630 + 1493.05 - 1} \\\\\n&\\approx 443.26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=444$ or larger."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171769 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171975 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0ba421f-3b4c-51da-86e6-ede62ea4437d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Sample Size When a Point Estimate Is Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "dffa0c20-14e5-5bdf-87b5-0b5818668999" :question/math-academy-id "e-15226" :question/problem "Consider a population of size $N=900$ where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Find a lower bound for the sample size $n$ required to ensure that the margin of error for the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having the characteristic is at most $0.06$.\n\n*Hint: Use a finite population correction factor. You may also use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ for a small population can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $N$ is the population size satisfying the condition $n > 0.05\\cdot N$,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}} \\leq \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nDoing some algebra to isolate $n$, we get\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\dfrac{Nm}{N + m - 1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nis the minimum sample size needed when the population is large.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = 0.025\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96\n$$\n\nWe do not know $\\widehat{\\,p}$, and consequently, we do not know $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ either. However, to find a lower bound for $n$, we can simply replace the expression $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with its maximum value for $\\widehat{\\,p} \\in [0,1]$.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ea9a3d120e0cd3907308a00182d86c94f1b8e23c61ec146e6b49c2ace93ea8.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the maximum value of $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ occurs when\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nand therefore, the maximum value of $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.06, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=\\dfrac 12, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}=1.96\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((1.96)^{2}0.25)/((0.06)^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 266.78, \\\\\n&= n \\mid \\ge \\frac{Nm}{N + m - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{900 \\cdot 266.78}{900 + 266.78 - 1} \\\\\n&\\approx 205.96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=206$ or larger."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171770 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171976 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "513da794-17bc-548a-ab5b-25ae6e3c4b15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum Sample Size When an Interval Estimate Is Given"}] :question/id #uuid "137029d8-2a73-56d0-b48d-0697b4af717e" :question/math-academy-id "e-15227" :question/problem "Consider a population of size $N=8\\,000$ where it's known that between $10\\%$ and $30\\%$ of individuals have a particular characteristic.\nFind a lower bound for sample size $n$ required to ensure that the margin of error for the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having the characteristic is at most $0.03$.\n\n*Hint: Use a finite population correction factor. You may also use the fact that $P(Z > 2.576) = 0.005$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ for a small population can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $N$ is the population size satisfying the condition $n > 0.05\\cdot N$,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nIf we require the margin of error to be at most $\\varepsilon$, then\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n} \\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}} \\leq \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nDoing some algebra to isolate $n$ we get\n\n$$\nn \\geq \\dfrac{Nm}{N + m - 1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nis the minimum sample size needed when the population is large.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.99=0.01 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.005\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.576) = 0.005\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.005} = 2.576\n$$\n\nWe have the estimate $p \\in [0.1, 0.3]$. So, to found our lower bound for $n$, we replace $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with the maximum of the function\n\n$$\nf(p) = p(1-p)\n$$\n\nfor $p\\in[0.1, 0.3]$.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1e7ed2808d6361f7fb5a1c3531e5233ebf76a2554f8f63097c5c0a0d4fb210.png)\n\nSince $f$ increases in the given interval, the maximum occurs at $p=0.3$. Therefore, we replace $\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})$ with\n\n$$\n0.3(1-0.3)=0.21\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon=0.03, \\qquad \\widehat{\\,p}=0.3, \\qquad z_{\\alpha/2}= 2.576\n$$\n\ninto the inequality for $n$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= (z_{α/2}^{2}p^(^)(1 - p^(^)))/(ε^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((2.576)^{2}0.3(1 - 0.3))/((0.03)^{2}) \\\\\n&\\approx 1548.35, \\\\\nn &= \\frac{Nm}{N + m - 1} \\\\\n&\\ge \\frac{8000 \\cdot 1548.35}{8000 + 1548.35 - 1} \\\\\n&= 1297.41\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounding up to the nearest integer, we get that $n=1298$ or larger."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171790 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171991 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2118c8-65aa-5078-9835-cbaf85d126c8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Expected Values"}] :question/id #uuid "27ac8318-fc79-594f-bd58-45ec5e5caacf" :question/math-academy-id "e-16492" :question/problem "A group of $80$ people, consisting of adults and children, were asked about their holiday preferences: beach or mountains. The aim is to determine whether people's age and vacation preferences are independent.\n\nThe following contingency table reports the sample's frequency distribution. Fill in the missing values of the expected frequencies under the hypothesis that the features are independent.\n\n|  | Beach | Mountains |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Adults | $O_{11}=20$<br>$E_{11} = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{26.4}}$ | $O_{12}=28$<br>$E_{12} =\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{21.6}}$ |\n| Children | $O_{21}=24$<br>$E_{21} = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{17.6}}$ | $O_{22}=8$<br>$E_{22} = \\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{14.4}}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are given a contingency table with $2$ levels for the row factor (Adults and Children) and $2$ for the column factors (Beach and Mountains).\n\nGiven a contingency table with $n$ levels for the row factor $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$ and $m$ levels for the column factor $B_1, B_2, \\ldots, B_m$, under the hypothesis that the factors are independent, we have that the probability of an individual having the value $A_i$ for the row factor and the value $B_j$ for the column factor is equal to the product of the probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(A_i \\cap B_j) = P(A_i) \\cdot P(B_j), \\qquad i=1,2,\\ldots, n \\quad j=1,2,\\ldots, m\n$$\n\nWe can calculate estimates for the probabilities $P(A_i)$ and $P(B_j)$, which we denote as $\\hat p(A_i)$ and $\\hat p(B_j)$, respectively, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^)(A_{i}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelA_{i}}{total number of observations} \\\\\np^(^)(B_{j}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelB_{j}}{total number of observations}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^)(Adults) &= \\frac{20 + 28}{80} = \\frac{3}{5} \\\\\np^(^)(Children) &= \\frac{24 + 8}{80} = \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\np^(^)(Beach) &= \\frac{20 + 24}{80} = \\frac{11}{20} \\\\\np^(^)(Mountains) &= \\frac{28 + 8}{80} = \\frac{9}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's estimate the probability of each combination of values under the assumption of independence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Adults∩Beach) &= p^(^)(Adults) \\cdot p^(^)(Beach) = \\frac{33}{100} \\\\\nP(Adults∩Mountains) &= p^(^)(Adults) \\cdot p^(^)(Mountains) = \\frac{27}{100} \\\\\nP(Children∩Beach) &= p^(^)(Children) \\cdot p^(^)(Beach) = \\frac{11}{50} \\\\\nP(Children∩Mountains) &= p^(^)(Children) \\cdot p^(^)(Mountains) = \\frac{9}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each combination of values under the assumption of independence can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{11} &= N \\cdot P(Adults∩Beach) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{33}{100} = 26.4 \\\\\nE_{12} &= N \\cdot P(Adults∩Mountains) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{27}{100} = 21.6 \\\\\nE_{21} &= N \\cdot P(Children∩Beach) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{11}{50} = 17.6 \\\\\nE_{22} &= N \\cdot P(Children∩Mountains) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{9}{50} = 14.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n|  | Beach | Mountains |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Adults | $O_{11}=20$<br>$E_{11} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}26.4}$ | $O_{12}=28$<br>$E_{12} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21.6}$ |\n| Children | $O_{21}=24$<br>$E_{21} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}17.6}$ | $O_{22}=8$<br>$E_{22} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}14.4}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171792 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112671c8-056c-5834-a297-179ad21b737c" :knowledge-point/title "Chi-Square Tests for Homogeneity"}] :question/id #uuid "6595e546-611d-5b6e-ac84-2638d9239f4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-16505" :question/problem "A research institute wants to determine whether there is a difference in voting preferences across two regions, Region A and Region B. They conducted two samples, one for each region, and asked each voter whether they planned to vote for Candidate X or Candidate Y in an upcoming election.\n\nIn total, $180$ voters were sampled from Region A, while $120$ were sampled from Region B. The following contingency table reports their findings.\n\n|  | Candidate X | Candidate Y |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Region A | $O_{11}=110$ | $O_{12}=70$ |\n| Region B | $O_{21}=70$ | $O_{22}=50$ |\n\nA chi-square test for homogeneity is conducted at the $10\\%$ significance level with the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n- $H_0:$: The distribution of voting preferences is the same across both regions\n\n- $H_1:$: The distribution of voting preferences is not the same across both regions\n\nThe table below gives the values of $w$ that satisfy $P(W\\geq w) = p$, where $W\\sim \\chi^2(\\nu)$.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $\\,2.706\\,$ | $\\,3.841\\,$ | $\\,5.024\\,$ | $\\,6.635\\,$ |\n| $2$ | $\\,4.605\\,$ | $\\,5.991\\,$ | $\\,7.378\\,$ | $\\,9.210\\,$ |\n| $3$ | $\\,6.251\\,$ | $\\,7.815\\,$ | $\\,9.348\\,$ | $\\,11.345\\,$ |\n\nUse this information to complete the hypothesis test and state your conclusion." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a contingency table in which a feature with $m$ levels is examined on $n$ samples from different populations. Then, the random variable\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i} \\sum_{j} \\frac{(O_{ij} - E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\n$$\n\napproximately follows a chi-square distribution with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $O_{ij}$ is the observed frequency for population $i$ and feature level $j$, and\n- $E_{ij}$ is the expected frequency for population $i$ and feature level $j$, as predicted under the null hypothesis that the distribution of the feature is the same in the two populations.\n\nGiven that $N_1, N_2, \\ldots, N_n$ are the sample sizes and\n\n$$\nN=N_1 + \\ldots + N_n\n$$\n\nis the grand sample size, the expected frequencies can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\nE_{ij} = N_i \\cdot \\widehat p_j\n$$\n\nwhere $\\widehat p_i$ is the proportion of individuals with value $A_i$ in the grand sample.\n\nThis approximation holds when all expected frequencies $E_{ij}$ are sufficiently large (typically\n\n$$\nE_{ij} \\geq 5\n$$\n\nfor each cell), and the observations are independent.\n\nIn our case, we have a sample of $N_1 = 180$ voters from Region A (population 1) and a sample of $N_2 = 120$ voters from Region B (population 2).\n\nFirst, we estimate the probability $\\widehat{p}_{X}$ of a randomly selected voter choosing Candidate X.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^)_{X} &= \\frac{110 + 70}{300} = 0.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the probability $\\widehat{p}_{Y}$ of that a randomly selected voter chooses Candidate Y is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^)_{Y} &= 1 - p^(^)_{X} = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each cell under the assumption that the distribution of votes is the same across both populations can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{11} &= p^(^)_{X} \\cdot N_{1} = 0.6 \\cdot 180 = 108 \\\\\nE_{12} &= p^(^)_{Y} \\cdot N_{1} = 0.4 \\cdot 180 = 72 \\\\\nE_{21} &= p^(^)_{X} \\cdot N_{2} = 0.6 \\cdot 120 = 72 \\\\\nE_{22} &= p^(^)_{Y} \\cdot N_{2} = 0.4 \\cdot 120 = 48\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our table now looks as follows:\n\n|  | Candidate X | Candidate Y |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Region A | $O_{11}\\|= 110; E_{11}\\|= 108$ | $O_{12}\\|= 70; E_{12}\\|= 72$ |\n| Region B | $O_{21}\\|= 70; E_{21}\\|= 72$ | $O_{22}\\|= 50; E_{22}\\|= 48$ |\n\nNotice that all of the expected frequencies are greater than or equal to $5$.\n\nNow, we calculate the term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(O_{ij} - E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\n$$\n\nfor each combination of value and population:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((O_{11} - E_{11})^{2})/(E_{11}) &= ((110 - 108)^{2})/(108) \\approx 0.0370 \\\\\n((O_{12} - E_{12})^{2})/(E_{12}) &= ((70 - 72)^{2})/(72) \\approx 0.0556 \\\\\n((O_{21} - E_{21})^{2})/(E_{21}) &= ((70 - 72)^{2})/(72) \\approx 0.0556 \\\\\n((O_{22} - E_{22})^{2})/(E_{22}) &= ((50 - 48)^{2})/(48) \\approx 0.0833\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we rounded to four decimal places.\n\nFirst, we compute our test statistic by summing the above results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= ∑_(i)∑_(j)((O_{ij} - E_{ij})^{2})/(E_{ij}) \\\\\n&= 0.0370 + 0.0556 + 0.0556 + 0.0833 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the test statistic is approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.2}$.\n\nNext, we compute the degrees of freedom:\n\n- Given a contingency table with $n$ populations and $m$ levels of a feature: the number of constraints is $1 + (m-1) + (n-1)$ the total number of observations must equal $N$, the estimates $\\widehat p_1, \\widehat p_2,\\ldots \\widehat p_{m-1}$ and $N_1, N_2, \\ldots N_{n-1}$ set a constraint each. Notice that the $\\widehat p_m$ and $N_n$ can be deduced by the others, so they don't create a constraint. Therefore, the number of degrees of freedom $\\nu$ is calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= number of cells - number of constraints \\\\\n&= nm - 1 - (n - 1) - (m - 1) \\\\\n&= nm - n - m + 1 \\\\\n&= (n - 1)(m - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case there is\n\n$$\n\\nu = (2-1)(2-1)=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}\n$$\n\ndegree of freedom.\n\nFrom the given chi-square table, the critical value for $\\nu=1$ at a $10\\%$ significance level is\n\n$$\n\\chi^2_{\\textrm{critical}}=2.706\n$$\n\nand the critical region is\n\n$$\nX\\geq 2.706\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcf4f6d388a82cbfcd89b77a0c441c2774aaee3a95a9f983fb6c484dbba37959.png)\n\nOur test statistic $(0.2)$ lies outside the critical region. Therefore, there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence to reject the null hypothesis.\n\nSo, we conclude that there is no evidence that the vote distribution varies across different regions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ac34ec-29b6-5020-92bf-b3c0a87a87ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.084" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523359} {:answer/id #uuid "49b3faa2-261e-5b23-a9d3-453a88a3fc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.126" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523405} {:answer/id #uuid "4d90ca59-9e8e-5ca6-9c41-334319a81f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\pm 0.205" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523413} {:answer/id #uuid "e39d1902-3b9f-5884-a0ff-1cb6fa586fa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\pm 0.152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523732} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9b0397-2df1-5c1b-bc5c-68b1434b1f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32ac34ec-29b6-5020-92bf-b3c0a87a87ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.084" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523359} :answer-field/id #uuid "e03129ca-31a0-5c5b-be36-c6ef3c14bdc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4c85404-18e8-5990-bb12-ba8d0d33ef92" :question/math-academy-id "q-142713" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=80$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.7,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since $n=80$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.75,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=80\\cdot 0.7=56>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=80\\cdot (1-0.7)=24>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.084\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533b2f03-04f0-5527-a86b-f93d974d8939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65\\pm 0.093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505270} {:answer/id #uuid "74b3b9e5-732e-55d4-bfcd-89de2017dcc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65\\pm 0.201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505340} {:answer/id #uuid "99f8fee4-71b0-5ee3-8216-6c083e12a604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505405} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff0b0f4-ca7f-5884-9640-7d13e7460657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65\\pm 0.052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505418} {:answer/id #uuid "a04b9b8d-7c34-521e-9936-d84df2c4f5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "533b2f03-04f0-5527-a86b-f93d974d8939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65\\pm 0.093" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505270} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb8692a1-74f0-5ff4-a1e6-f0adf87799e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b09786c-003f-565a-9fd2-fe6264490f01" :question/math-academy-id "q-142715" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $65%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since $n=100$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=65%=0.65,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=100\\cdot 0.65=65>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=100\\cdot (1-0.65)=35>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\n![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.65\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.65(1-0.65)}{100}} \\\\  & 0.65\\pm 0.093\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51bb8808-acc1-5bbe-be4c-18bb2a65467d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505266} {:answer/id #uuid "64697aae-ec23-5ab7-a338-517c91ef2dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.24\\pm 0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505306} {:answer/id #uuid "a10aa168-0f6b-5c3e-b6d3-52b5f81ecea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.017" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505422} {:answer/id #uuid "bf990066-e8e2-556a-a296-fcaab89dd3be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.24\\pm 0.091" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505480} {:answer/id #uuid "d30ed7ea-b91a-59ba-890e-8f565053e4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d30ed7ea-b91a-59ba-890e-8f565053e4bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505510} :answer-field/id #uuid "8362fec0-95c3-51e3-8cbd-6c9d9645d33c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42e7a19f-f4f3-5e6a-9979-741a2870cc54" :question/math-academy-id "q-142726" :question/problem "A survey of $150$ randomly selected students at a particular university showed that only $24$ select at least one math course. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the proportion of students from the entire university who select at least one math course. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of the students at a particular university,\n\n- some of the students select at least one math course, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=150,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who select at least one math course can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{24}{150}=0.16\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=150\\cdot 0.16=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=150\\cdot (1-0.16)=126>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.16\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.16(1-0.16)}{150}} \\\\  & 0.16\\pm 0.059\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afc3c2ae-2cc1-5bf4-9091-7e8ce0b36fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168880} {:answer/id #uuid "f34ad498-79e7-537d-b10a-0f37546440b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5 ± 1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168881} {:answer/id #uuid "fd932c61-ca06-5855-84a6-0db3a29de204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168882} {:answer/id #uuid "716b53f5-d73e-512e-a456-e3d78c8f50ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5 ± 1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168883} {:answer/id #uuid "7a612825-1be2-5593-9303-88c57b254d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 ± 2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168884}] :answer-field/id #uuid "10da2cf7-6d37-52fb-b88a-de3b14b6f05f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168879}] :question/id #uuid "91e7efeb-2080-5fcf-90b5-7519fc7fd2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143249" :question/problem "Let $y^(^) = β^(^)_{0} + β^(^)_{1}x = 3.5 + 5x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_{1}, y_{1}), …, (x_{20}, y_{20})$ of $n = 20$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $8$, and $S_{xx} = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} = 24$, find a $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.101) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $18$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $β^(^)_{1}$ is given by $SE[β^(^)_{1}] = \\sqrt{\\frac{MSE}{S_{xx}}}$.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "555a7349-2df2-538a-a305-59c88e1a33fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554033} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5a082e-ad3d-51f6-8adc-48f4994128fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554092} {:answer/id #uuid "755634f2-688a-5a80-a8c4-78381f6a4f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554107} {:answer/id #uuid "790f4579-99a1-5019-8dd1-e9c8506783f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554113} {:answer/id #uuid "8344100f-13ac-5416-8e25-9c878812b0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f5a082e-ad3d-51f6-8adc-48f4994128fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554092} :answer-field/id #uuid "10137e41-2459-5b06-b3a4-3230a1046efa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ac4a55b-9276-584c-aabe-f3000169b367" :question/math-academy-id "q-143260" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** | **$9$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Height ($y$)** | $95$ | $106$ | $115$ | $125$ |\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of four children. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=80.6+5x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=6,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.35,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>4.303)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=80.6+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=80.6+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>4.303)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.025}=4.303.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=4.303\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.35}{20}}≈0.6\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=5\\pm 0.6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa3363d-ab33-5aa9-af84-d3b6a64ef0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.40\\pm 0.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523306} {:answer/id #uuid "25aeefc7-0d24-5ed3-a18a-7d64cdb2b831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32\\pm 0.054" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523328} {:answer/id #uuid "4c12b846-20ca-5213-95bb-ffa4ca0fbb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.64\\pm 0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523409} {:answer/id #uuid "e296f059-0c25-50c1-9765-f7943410911b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523729} {:answer/id #uuid "f5dd3fe5-4f40-50ef-9d86-6ca6a32afafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32\\pm 0.107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5dd3fe5-4f40-50ef-9d86-6ca6a32afafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.32\\pm 0.107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523765} :answer-field/id #uuid "537466c1-89bf-5f49-8a56-e0b12cdda1be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a59a953-fd66-5704-998f-ee025eba0b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143305" :question/problem "A survey conducted by a sports magazine on a random sample of $125$ students from various U.S. universities reported that $40$ respondents love soccer. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the national proportion of college students who love soccer. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all college students in the US,\n\n- some of the students surveyed love soccer, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who love soccer can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{40}{125}=0.32\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=125\\cdot 0.32=40>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=125\\cdot (1-0.32)=85>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.32\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.32(1-0.32)}{125}} \\\\  & 0.32\\pm 0.107\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7c6c7fd-c206-5d2b-9bb8-212b149cc58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 ± 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169024} {:answer/id #uuid "d825665e-b8aa-5139-a7cb-926db46a3125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 ± 1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169025} {:answer/id #uuid "e92e59ff-5e3e-5c77-95b4-162b62dc9d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 ± 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169026} {:answer/id #uuid "74d053f3-6596-55aa-973c-e4e0b579f127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 ± 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169027} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad85fa4-2edd-5e14-abdc-4965116e5ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 ± 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169028}] :answer-field/id #uuid "176b1224-4149-5ce1-b343-d39ae618cb85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169023}] :question/id #uuid "25698e55-2308-5356-a383-ee209257efc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159411" :question/problem "Let $y^(^) = β^(^)_{0} + β^(^)_{1}x =-3.1 + 2x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_{1}, y_{1}), …, (x_{12}, y_{12})$ of $n = 12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $0.95$, and $S_{xx} = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} = 143$, find a $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.228) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $β^(^)_{1}$ is given by $SE[β^(^)_{1}] = \\sqrt{\\frac{MSE}{S_{xx}}}$.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbd2b70-b3ee-509d-be90-3ff4a1a62f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553922} {:answer/id #uuid "26a57e1e-5b7b-57c8-aa04-88dc12913fd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.01" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553941} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac845ba-d2be-5059-8e0d-b39565e56bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554164} {:answer/id #uuid "b12dd743-8c8e-57ed-8222-bc18a8d6084f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554261} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b413f8-d2fb-5f5c-af45-47160c32ee73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ac845ba-d2be-5059-8e0d-b39565e56bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1\\pm 0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554164} :answer-field/id #uuid "618d8cc5-09ee-52cf-bb55-128c57e46ade" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d548f76-b08c-5cf0-ae75-59db6390b2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159571" :question/problem "| ** Production  $\\,$ ($x$)** | **$24$** | **$27$** | **$30$** | **$31$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ** Price per $kg$  ($y$) ** | $2$ | $1.6$ | $1.4$ | $1.4$ |\n\nThe table above shows wheat production, in tons, and the average price of a kilogram of flour, in dollars, for a given country over the last four years. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=4-0.1x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=28,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.007,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.   \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-4-0.1x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=4-0.1x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(24-28)^{2}+(27-28)^{2}+(30-28)^{2}+(31-28)^{2} \\\\  & =16+1+4+9 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.007}{30}}≈0.04\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.1\\pm 0.04\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171973 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "918f0c69-95cd-59c7-a397-d566b585a088" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Minimum Sample Size Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a79cfc2b-ff1c-5592-9b85-85f37fa713cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170240} {:answer/id #uuid "d4dd4556-bbc8-51c8-9747-a56dc8452f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170241} {:answer/id #uuid "08270179-502e-557b-a631-df612856d337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170242} {:answer/id #uuid "232fdf93-dd2d-5e7c-9ac1-69be18e3de39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170243} {:answer/id #uuid "63220fb9-9655-56c5-9dc4-97f7cadc0843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170244}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5a00ae23-d196-503f-8984-6016da7d7097" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170239} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "daf62179-f5d0-519d-b89b-e3cab6e9cf27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499