the two samples.\n\nYou may use the following percentage points table for the normal distribution.\n\n| $z$ | $0.95$ | $0.975$ | $0.98$ | $0.99$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $Φ^{-1}(p)$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.054$ | $2.326$ |\n\n$n_{2}$ must be {{field-1}} then $n_{1}$ by a factor of approximately {{field-2}}.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171980 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a8e4b4-e8e0-5115-8928-d2809c34a16e" :knowledge-point/title "Conducting a One-Tailed Hypothesis for Two Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83e7a14d-6762-57f5-b0da-809b305a1844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170412} {:answer/id #uuid "fe55f50d-f53b-5f41-bcf2-41b37ec39542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "insufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170413}] :answer-field/id #uuid "255e0158-8dea-537f-8fb0-b3c9a7dd06ad" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170411} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85083b1a-4d37-5a2e-8f47-b860544e64c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.282" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170415} {:answer/id #uuid "b98dcfb4-fce1-5033-b7cc-8791b0dbea9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.282" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170416} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f5f0de-e0be-52d2-8ca7-8a5d1751df4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170417} {:answer/id #uuid "cda64c84-16c5-5e32-b178-a7c531b03587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170418} {:answer/id #uuid "38d3a7ce-cf49-5652-ae97-500498f727fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170419}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1f56fe70-8145-5c97-9177-a83810e6353e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170414}] :question/id #uuid "cf8bf231-46bf-5fdd-bd7a-e9fb04a34584" :question/math-academy-id "q-251508" :question/problem "Consider two samples of sizes $n_{1} = 200$ and $n_{2} = 80$ from two large populations where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the samples are independent and the proportions of individuals having the characteristic in the two samples are $p^(^)_{1} = 0.2$ and $p^(^)_{2} = 0.3$, respectively, we wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the proportion $p_{1}$ of individuals having the characteristic in the first population is smaller than the corresponding proportion $p_{2}$ in the second population.\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nThe table above shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$, where $Z∼N(0, 1)$. Using this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ **left-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\le$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nAt the $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-2}}\nevidence that $p_{1} < p_{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171980 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9a8e4b4-e8e0-5115-8928-d2809c34a16e" :knowledge-point/title "Conducting a One-Tailed Hypothesis for Two Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a02b107-8018-5c8b-bd0c-ee5d0c814105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.326" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170463} {:answer/id #uuid "b25c364e-98be-5181-81aa-cdefa47b08a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170464} {:answer/id #uuid "037d7420-7309-590d-9d78-adfbea9d1e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170465} {:answer/id #uuid "41807fcc-94f5-55f5-b5c9-afe5546598e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.282" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170466} {:answer/id #uuid "5694e8cf-3540-55eb-948c-71a059b149a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170467}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1dfc8f47-9ebf-5d7e-a41e-08d6a109686c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170462} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0cb9424-41f4-5c58-8424-bec2f1869d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "insufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170469} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cb25d7-0f35-5774-b440-3fae556183f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170470}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ac9a67bc-f263-51dc-b33b-8e0ab515c6f8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170468}] :question/id #uuid "21d85203-55f1-573a-a6b6-2b86287a1932" :question/math-academy-id "q-251568" :question/problem "Consider two samples of sizes $n_{1} = 80$ and $n_{2} = 70$ from two large populations where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the samples are independent and the proportions of individuals having the characteristic in the two samples are $p^(^)_{1} = 0.35$ and $p^(^)_{2} = 0.2$, respectively, we wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the proportion $p_{1}$ of individuals having the characteristic in the first population is larger than the corresponding proportion $p_{2}$ in the second population.\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nThe table above shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$, where $Z∼N(0, 1)$. Using this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ **right-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\ge$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nAt the $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-2}}\nevidence that $p_{1} > p_{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171982 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee47ba92-4c21-5c4d-a73b-288487285f39" :knowledge-point/title "Testing a Hypothesis for Differences in Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc54eaa2-e5f1-569b-85db-a99fb1eee730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.282" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170478} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9cec7b-a7b6-5e08-9097-a95ef85dd8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.326" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170479} {:answer/id #uuid "49d902fd-f1d1-5477-96b6-259c6d92679a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170480} {:answer/id #uuid "5a250824-573d-554b-aef3-5655773ef172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170481} {:answer/id #uuid "682a21df-0b88-5628-a49c-9ce162f5491f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170482}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2c299faa-5d0f-5da2-86d1-a9a891445448" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3220cfb5-a565-5dbf-88e9-9f05d1f7b2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "insufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170484} {:answer/id #uuid "6c64b7a5-fa44-58f9-bdeb-9c71e4251d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170485}] :answer-field/id #uuid "edb4d6f8-954c-52c5-a84e-38b293dce40a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170483}] :question/id #uuid "77b223c9-354b-50fa-9a54-915a03edd98d" :question/math-academy-id "q-251615" :question/problem "A pharmaceutical company wants to test if the proportion of patients adhering to their new medication regimen is higher in Clinic A than in Clinic B. The summary statistics of two samples from each clinic are given in the table below, where $n$ is the sample size of the surveyed patients, and $p^(^)$ is the proportion of patients who adhere to the regimen in the respective sample.\n\n|  | Clinic A | Clinic B |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $400$ | $350$ |\n| $p^(^)$ | $0.815$ | $0.74$ |\n\nA hypothesis test is conducted at the $1 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the proportion $p_{1}$ of patients adhering to the medication regimen in Clinic A is greater than the corresponding proportion $p_{2}$ in Clinic B.\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nThe table above shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$, where $Z∼N(0, 1)$. Using this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe $1 \\mathrm{\\% }$ **right-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\ge$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nAt the $1 \\mathrm{\\% }$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-2}}\nevidence that $p_{1} > p_{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171981 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e04359e6-de3a-5804-b6d4-47657634cf63" :knowledge-point/title "Conducting a Two-Tailed Hypothesis for Two Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a99bef15-a86b-599a-9594-87dbb8b2a8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170574} {:answer/id #uuid "f88a44ae-7263-5cab-83a9-220006c7a319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.326" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170575} {:answer/id #uuid "12a9c3bb-f22b-5f66-acdd-36e08024cfd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170576} {:answer/id #uuid "18afdb17-f0f2-5b3c-9f85-19b401185678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170577} {:answer/id #uuid "273287bd-83d4-5b78-9772-c81656e48629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.282" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170578}] :answer-field/id #uuid "78bf49ad-04bc-55ff-a4ce-8f7aeea6077e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170573} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5c618ed-ac10-54b8-bf26-16ff178016ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170580} {:answer/id #uuid "db4c4ed9-fe65-5aa6-b401-a479d0b20f7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170581} {:answer/id #uuid "6083b0d7-3ce7-5ba7-b9a4-2119893fbd25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.326" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170582} {:answer/id #uuid "730ce093-1d29-52c3-a628-dd1feab03e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.282" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170583} {:answer/id #uuid "79dcafb5-face-514c-8b6f-9bb3ac48c201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170584}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9d83e147-fb92-57e9-9f9b-151d1b4c94fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d8a760-a0f8-57e1-bedd-f3eff3d25f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "sufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170586} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1c3af6-210e-5c5c-9e70-e57921631325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "insufficient" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170587}] :answer-field/id #uuid "1eef5079-3725-598f-a94e-ebb881343ef1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170585}] :question/id #uuid "6b0cf637-138f-5243-94cb-14a938845c8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-251758" :question/problem "Consider two samples of sizes $n_{1} = 130$ and $n_{2} = 110$ from two large populations where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the samples are independent and the proportions of individuals having the characteristic in the two samples are $p^(^)_{1} = 0.5$ and $p^(^)_{2} = 0.4$, respectively, we wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the proportion $p_{1}$ of individuals having the characteristic in the first population does not equal to the corresponding proportion $p_{2}$ in the second population.\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nThe table above shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$, where $Z∼N(0, 1)$. Using this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ **two-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately\n$-1.960$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\nAt the $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-3}}\nevidence that $p_{1} \\ne p_{2}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175750 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c32f307-f4d3-5327-82d4-0bbfc8ea6132" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Estimate for the Variance"}] :question/id #uuid "235ab2b8-322b-550f-9154-4712ce887d1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-16376" :question/problem "Consider a normal population with mean $\\mu = 10$ and unknown variance. An independent random sample is drawn from the population, and it is found that approximately $95\\%$ of the data in the sample have a value between $4$ and $16$. Using the empirical rule for the normal distribution, find an estimate of the population variance." :question/worked-solution "According to the empirical rule, we have the following:\n\n- Approximately $68\\%$ of the data falls within $1$ standard deviation of the mean.\n- Approximately $95\\%$ of the data falls within $2$ standard deviations of the mean.\n- Approximately $99.7\\%$ of the data falls within $3$ standard deviations of the mean.\n\nWe can assume that $100\\%$ of the data falls within $4$ (or more) standard deviations of the mean.\n\n![](images/5f/5fb2ab2c716e1d7886c6859c2f172d606b3896a375b249c3a2fcfc340b5f230e.png)\n\nSo, according to this rule, $95\\%$ of the observations of a random normal variable $N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)$ fall in the interval $(\\mu-2\\sigma, \\mu+2\\sigma)$. This range represents 4 standard deviations. Therefore, we can write:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ - 2σ^(^) &= 4 \\\\\nμ + 2σ^(^) &= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have used $\\widehat{\\sigma}$ since this is an *estimate* of the population standard deviation $\\sigma$.\n\nSolving for $\\widehat{\\sigma}$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\sigma} = \\dfrac{16-4}{4} = 3\n$$\n\nFinally, the estimate of the population variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\sigma}^2 = 3^2 = 9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175888 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e77a14c-9943-5494-ae89-a77c7a1888d0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Residuals From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "bc4500bc-8992-5148-95eb-6f84ae2508cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9717" :question/problem "![](images/08/089aa0d861b267352f9e5d83f47a00d5fd330f0f280268783e5a8e88cf947a98.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above. According to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Residual} = \\text{Actual} - \\textrm{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(82,70).$ So, our actual value is $y=70.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=82$ has the $y$-coordinate $65.$ So, our estimated value is $\\widehat{y}=65.$\n\n![](images/99/99e3741f602802e636e2e49ef755954343079b8c0d98453dac9364ddc08ef16f.png)\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =70-65 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175889 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0286d36b-217f-52e4-8580-e031b6dea31e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residuals Using the Regression Line Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "cb2190d0-5150-53ff-b7ab-ed364611bc8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9718" :question/problem "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=0.5x+7.\n$$\n\nAccording to the model, what is the residual at $x=6$ if the actual value of $y$ corresponding to this value of $x$ is $12?$" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Residual} = \\text{Actual} - \\textrm{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =0.5x+7 \\\\  & =0.5(6)+7 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =12-10 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0286d36b-217f-52e4-8580-e031b6dea31e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residuals Using the Regression Line Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7198df-fc16-57b1-a77a-b9edff234f9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174302} {:answer/id #uuid "640b6612-5cd5-5b2b-b186-165710224e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554063} {:answer/id #uuid "66ae108f-494f-57ed-a796-8e17686429c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$(-2\\,500)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554072} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb39097-7715-5a68-b897-b0f54a11856e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554080} {:answer/id #uuid "844c502d-5d46-511c-9e11-95d3245ec04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$(-1\\,500)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "640b6612-5cd5-5b2b-b186-165710224e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554063} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd589229-6d62-561a-8ccf-4b0128261c8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a189cf42-a35d-541c-8fa9-b13012c26ed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-134266" :question/problem "An antique broker has records about the value of an artwork $y$ (in thousands of dollars) at certain times $x$ (in decades) since the artwork was produced. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=3.2x+4.5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the residual $5$ decades after the artwork was produced if the actual value of the artwork after $5$ decades is $$23\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the estimated value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =3.2x+4.5 \\\\  & =3.2(5)+4.5 \\\\  & =20.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =23-20.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $$2\\,500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e77a14c-9943-5494-ae89-a77c7a1888d0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Residuals From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf731189-32fe-57a4-bde5-3c54274327ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174305} {:answer/id #uuid "1a6379b8-ece0-5a99-844b-b7f486334f38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174306} {:answer/id #uuid "441aa6e9-1299-567b-858c-691df1df7a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174307} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4cb6ac-32c3-5dd7-9a8f-634d8cdf9d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174308} {:answer/id #uuid "621b8204-3932-59dd-b595-a75ccb8e4c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b4cb6ac-32c3-5dd7-9a8f-634d8cdf9d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174308} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5802a2f-64ed-5fd4-b9cc-65c0cef24d68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd98bac0-93e8-506d-a8ba-d1595548f912" :question/math-academy-id "q-134267" :question/problem "![](images/a7/a7fb89105bc5a11837ed0f416b2327d88202e8e782fa4c114ac47b3d5d15421c.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(7,-2).$ So, our actual value is $y=-2.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=7$ has the $y$-coordinate $-4.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=-4.$\n\n![](images/92/92a26b8ab019879dec3208878f6a0604c0616776b09e3f8fa916fcab72df3a73.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =-2-(-4) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184092 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "897ae010-a9a2-5971-a1ec-c2618cb8ad2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Population Variance From Sample Data"}] :question/id #uuid "a50a0dd2-10b1-5984-ae1b-6198de3f44ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-7880" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n5, \\quad 3, \\quad 6, \\quad 4, \\quad 7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right] .\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ and\n\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = s^2$ denotes the unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(5+3+6+4+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{135}{5} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =27-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $2.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184093 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb182688-828c-5252-8d6d-d09c93ba2bc8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance Using the Alternative Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "86da2f2b-8028-5807-ba02-63722c4c0f61" :question/math-academy-id "e-7881" :question/problem "Suppose that $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_9$ is a random sample of size $n=9$ from a population, and \n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nCompute an unbiased estimate of the population variance." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right] .\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ and\n\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = s^2$ denotes an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ that's computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nHere, there are $n=9$ data points in the sample, and we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{8} \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184112 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aa1599e-b9c0-5282-ac58-b10211cb7d30" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance"}] :question/id #uuid "b3c0e272-0059-59e6-b3ea-55aaecb14cda" :question/math-academy-id "e-9921" :question/problem "A random sample of size $n=81$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{81} \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 180\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere each $x_i$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population standard deviation." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$ and population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then the sample variance $S^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{n}{n-1} \\left[ \\overline{X^2} - \\left( \\overline{X} \\right)^2 \\right] .\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance $S^2$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ and\n\n- $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = s^2$ denotes an unbiased estimate of $\\sigma^2$ that's computed from the sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nWe're given that $\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{81} \\left( x_i - \\overline{x} \\right)^2 = 180$ and $n=81.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81-1}\\cdot 180 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{80}\\cdot 180 \\\\  & =2.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $\\hat{\\sigma}^2  = 2.25.$ We take the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\hat{\\sigma} = \\sqrt{2.25} = 1.5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "897ae010-a9a2-5971-a1ec-c2618cb8ad2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Population Variance From Sample Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba80ffcf-73eb-5f1a-8fd4-d63450f1bfa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183830} {:answer/id #uuid "e450345a-e368-5b38-aab7-b820247601a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183831} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b6517e-8f2a-5f23-8e22-b1018a73b77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183832} {:answer/id #uuid "18814d35-1a72-50a7-a1ca-7d133e3fff5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183833} {:answer/id #uuid "67109040-4d97-57d2-9184-2956dfd755e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8b6517e-8f2a-5f23-8e22-b1018a73b77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183832} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf68f810-83c4-5c31-ab5d-3a95adffba29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fd4e806-e213-5cdb-aa92-418bede47190" :question/math-academy-id "q-88857" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n4,\\,8,\\,10,\\,6,\\,7\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(4+8+10+6+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({4}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{10}^{2}+{6}^{2}+{7}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{265}{5} \\\\  & =53.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =53-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188194 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/id #uuid "5614b56d-26e1-579c-8b19-9c667c68b385" :question/math-academy-id "e-15152" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=84$ from a population where $42\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $50\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9292 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9306 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9319 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9418 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9429 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9441 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9525 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9535 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9545 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np > 5,$ and $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n = 84,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p = 0.42.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp = 84(0.42) = 35.28 > 5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p) = 84(1-0.42) = 48.72 > 5,\n$$\n\nand $n=84$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{84}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,0.0029).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\widehat{\\,p} > 0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.5) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.5-0.42}{\\sqrt{0.0029}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.49) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.49) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.49).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.49) & =0.9319.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.5) & =1-Φ(1.49) \\\\  & =1-0.9319 \\\\  & =0.0681.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188195 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/id #uuid "dac77d50-5617-55c6-9824-37afb60cf639" :question/math-academy-id "e-15153" :question/problem "A popular streaming service has a database of $100$ million songs, of which $64$ million have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. If a user randomly creates a playlist with $128$ songs, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $64$ and $96$ (inclusive) songs in the sample have a duration of less than $3$ minutes. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.4 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0003 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.3 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0005 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, -3.2 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0007 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.0006 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.07 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.08 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.09 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.5 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9949 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9951 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9952 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.6 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9962 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9963 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9964 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.7 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9972 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9973 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9974 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np > 5,$ and $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n = 128,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p = \\dfrac{64\\,000\\,000}{100\\,000\\,000} = 0.64.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp = 128(0.64) = 81.92 > 5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p) = 128(1-0.64) = 46.08 > 5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=128$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.64,\\frac{0.64(1-0.64)}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.64,0.0018).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $64$ and $96$ of the songs in the playlist last less than $3$ minutes, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{64}{128}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{96}{128} \\\\ 0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.5 \\leq          \\widehat{\\,p} \\leq          0.75).$\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.75) & =P(\\frac{0.5-0.64}{\\sqrt{0.0018}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.75-0.64}{\\sqrt{0.0018}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-3.30\\le Z\\le 2.59) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.59)-P(Z\\le -3.30) \\\\  & =Φ(2.59)-Φ(-3.30)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.59)=0.9952,\\,Φ(-3.30)=0.0005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.75) & =Φ(2.59)-Φ(-3.30) \\\\  & =0.9952-0.0005 \\\\  & =0.9947.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cff8c97-4bc8-566d-ad66-106ac6e6df7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186349} {:answer/id #uuid "fcef61bf-8275-505a-a891-3cdd0ead337c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186350} {:answer/id #uuid "589e4922-86da-5c4c-a370-cd10f1dedaf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186351} {:answer/id #uuid "78e24531-10e7-52e0-b301-70505aef56b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186352} {:answer/id #uuid "7fcab255-bd19-5c59-a90c-bc05aac1bce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186353}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a30e09be-77ef-52ab-9621-291c7738b9f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186348}] :question/id #uuid "90fadc79-6d06-5385-8518-5436dbdfb1f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-146152" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $p^(^)$ be approximated as\n$p^(^)∼N(p, (p(1 - p))/(n))$,\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\n1. A sample from a class of $24$ students, where $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the students in the school are right-handed.\n2. A sample of $120$ company employees, where $60 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the company employees are right-handed.\n3. A sample of $35$ players in a team, $70 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the players in the league are right-handed.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d1f49e2-0e08-5966-bf0b-33d79bc46bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.66)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512316} {:answer/id #uuid "873cc424-c085-5723-b881-20ef7fecbd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.52)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512413} {:answer/id #uuid "b6c0529c-fbdc-5cfe-88eb-01281ed4b7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.58)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512534} {:answer/id #uuid "e538117d-c9dc-58a0-8c48-4a9c7a9e89b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.53)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512646} {:answer/id #uuid "ee346f5b-62c5-52d8-9668-4cfe0bea6f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6c0529c-fbdc-5cfe-88eb-01281ed4b7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(0.58)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512534} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab3a112-abbd-5b74-bf90-3997e193c1b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0faf39ad-2709-5ec7-94d0-99312772d48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146155" :question/problem "A nationwide study found that $38%$ of families own a pet. If a town randomly samples $200$ families, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $80$ of them are pet owners. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=200,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.38.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=200(0.38)=76>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=200(1-0.38)=124>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=200$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,\\frac{0.38(1-0.38)}{200}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,0.0012).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $80$ families in the sample own a pet, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}>\\frac{80}{200}=0.4.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.38}{\\sqrt{0.0012}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.58) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.58) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2dfdd82-65a5-5171-bc01-7821fbf0698e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1118" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186367} {:answer/id #uuid "c39133e9-a888-5d29-b93c-9e5408b6d72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3853" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186368} {:answer/id #uuid "1356a3ec-56fc-5170-816e-abe9552e7e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186369} {:answer/id #uuid "2abc7d34-8251-5017-899a-0156b06b5501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1846" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186370} {:answer/id #uuid "7e69b089-b6f5-5b27-9c28-5a5089665d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0835" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1356a3ec-56fc-5170-816e-abe9552e7e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2605" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186369} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd8ca12c-8970-56fc-9321-068eac62971b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da01a854-bda8-5ccb-8dc9-1deaf2392385" :question/math-academy-id "q-146160" :question/problem "In a school of $900$ students, $432$ of the students are male. If the school randomly samples $50$ students, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $20$ and $23$ (inclusive) of students in the sample are male. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1093\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1292\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1539\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1515\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3859\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4247\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{432}{900}=0.48.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.48)=24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.48)=26>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.48,\\frac{0.48(1-0.48)}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.48,0.0050).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $20$ and $23$ of students in the sample are male, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{50}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{23}{50} \\\\ 0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.46).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.46) & =P(\\frac{0.4-0.48}{\\sqrt{0.0050}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.46-0.48}{\\sqrt{0.0050}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.13\\le Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.28)-P(Z\\le -1.13) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.28)-Φ(-1.13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.28)=0.3897,\\,Φ(-1.13)=0.1292.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.46) & =Φ(-0.28)-Φ(-1.13) \\\\  & =0.3897-0.1292 \\\\  & =0.2605.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ff42d1-7d91-59db-9ac4-7c3a9e11dfe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.34)-Φ(-1.34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516227} {:answer/id #uuid "6092f662-75ed-5f8a-9564-15a7e43b742f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.28)-Φ(-1.28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516322} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7d0474-7aea-50f9-ae3c-3081a543503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.98)-Φ(-0.98)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516546} {:answer/id #uuid "cef03ff5-191a-5d70-bca6-cb830b7fda60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516552} {:answer/id #uuid "ffaee5fa-f5c5-56f1-b2fb-18822e27e724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.72)-Φ(-0.72)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef03ff5-191a-5d70-bca6-cb830b7fda60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516552} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7552ed-0a05-5263-8474-d257c2707a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b040f12-bfb6-5271-9a10-66bca5c20f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-146163" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $55%$ and $65%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=100,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.6.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=100(0.6)=60>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=100(1-0.6)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=100$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65) & =P(\\frac{0.55-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.65-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.02\\le Z\\le 1.02) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.02)-P(Z\\le -1.02) \\\\  & =Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8383fc80-11b0-5ef7-a4ff-98907dac9895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186385} {:answer/id #uuid "f3dfffd9-756c-54ff-9fad-72bb1b97c8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186386} {:answer/id #uuid "3f023834-d3ab-5a26-93f8-ddbe345ca7b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9747" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186387} {:answer/id #uuid "6fed7bd2-bde0-5e7d-9d0e-d101acece082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186388} {:answer/id #uuid "788ac932-3ccb-52cf-888f-ee298f7621e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9319" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fed7bd2-bde0-5e7d-9d0e-d101acece082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186388} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b4a4235-f096-5b72-a48d-8a38aff78371" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbed22eb-e3d1-5c23-b166-31d76c217f5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146165" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $42%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $35%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0735\\,$ | $\\,0.0721\\,$ | $\\,0.0708\\,$ | $\\,0.0694\\,$ | $\\,0.0681\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ | $\\,0.0869\\,$ | $\\,0.0853\\,$ | $\\,0.0838\\,$ | $\\,0.0823\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ | $\\,0.1038\\,$ | $\\,0.1020\\,$ | $\\,0.1003\\,$ | $\\,0.0985\\,$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.42.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.42)=33.6>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.42)=46.4>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.42,0.003\\,045).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.35).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.35) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.35-0.42}{\\sqrt{0.003\\,045}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-1.27) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-1.27) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-1.27).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.27) & =0.1020.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.35) & =1-Φ(-1.27) \\\\  & =1-0.1020 \\\\  & =0.8980.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3601da7-a7c3-52cd-8f70-f617dbff7130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186391} {:answer/id #uuid "dccc85bb-7f5c-57a0-98aa-12945003cf5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186392} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d07aea-24ea-5f53-9672-04c6dad6b1f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186393} {:answer/id #uuid "68126574-febc-56bd-96e3-be660cd1d396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186394} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8c475d-4fb2-5d19-8a64-6b6d5fd924f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3601da7-a7c3-52cd-8f70-f617dbff7130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186391} :answer-field/id #uuid "0083b82a-9efc-51d0-860f-723f4347222c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6270c9-4286-526b-a383-94a2b499f6f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-146169" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=16$ from a population of size $N=100$ in which $25%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=32$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $10%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=32$  from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $75%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =16,\\, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & <16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.1) \\\\  & =3.2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.75) \\\\  & =24 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =32(1-0.75) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f29f42a-f248-5ef7-b9ac-f920b72c82d4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862c2d98-ad8b-512d-bfb2-274840043d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045