e "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98722bfc-8be6-5c8a-a81b-1c2e44fb3762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68 ± 0.037" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187537} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7129ce-460b-5ac9-bcb9-e1e3a4d2630e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68 ± 0.056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187538} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d4e00f-9c65-5a0a-bae8-7aa9486c0111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68 ± 0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187539} {:answer/id #uuid "1aea5fc9-8d42-55a5-becc-9f1af934af6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68 ± 0.074" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187540} {:answer/id #uuid "452c2b7e-f89d-520a-95c4-652d87f46ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68 ± 0.047" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187541}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa4fc8f-9c64-54de-8770-587a03ab6a02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187536}] :question/id #uuid "2b9241a4-8c2e-5f88-84e9-822404fa5522" :question/math-academy-id "q-251431" :question/problem "![](images/39/391592a451e201eb026c1df951319c7ba6915d9650a6b31e8d7e7ef52d932e1a.png)\n\nA total of $N = 800$ customers of a company used a delivery service. In a random sample of $n = 200$ of these customers, a proportion of $p^(^) = 0.68$ declared they were satisfied with the delivery service.\n\nFind a $90 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of customers who are satisfied with the delivery service.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05$, where $Z∼N(0, 1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b4ae633-d836-59a7-8030-002829769a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5\\pm 3.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553976} {:answer/id #uuid "a18bd7cb-4599-5047-a357-7726837ec776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5\\pm 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554226} {:answer/id #uuid "a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5\\pm 2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554248} {:answer/id #uuid "ede2b151-7a0f-5a99-a1ae-0b5aa02441f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554389} {:answer/id #uuid "f48cd6d6-78c5-50fe-84ac-c81348f46ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9cd2e1a-8327-5ae8-8f04-0b0a204554bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5\\pm 2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554248} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f9df6ed-4f2d-5912-b30b-a8d79df169ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186189760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68d56eaf-9e11-54bc-9fcb-5131b64bc995" :question/math-academy-id "q-143256" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{20},{y}_{20})$ of $n=20$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2,$ the mean square error of the model is $8,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=24,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.101)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $18$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.5+5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.5+5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=3.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.101)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{18,0.025}=2.101.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.101\\cdot \\sqrt{8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{24})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.5\\pm 2.8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6dfb82b-0623-5a54-9e30-26de68114a6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer