regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 3.5$, and $MSE = 6.1$, find a $90 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n$SE[β^(^)_{0}] = \\sqrt{MSE \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5808b20-c34c-5b3e-9a48-0d95ce62906e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5 ± 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191021} {:answer/id #uuid "a9577506-20b7-585a-84ef-21c6634c7a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8 ± 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191022} {:answer/id #uuid "ddcb9a82-948a-5fad-b912-20abbd43b295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5 ± 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191023} {:answer/id #uuid "e2947aed-c93c-5bf3-b1e8-733117e2d50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8 ± 1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191024} {:answer/id #uuid "30d86245-2075-5d90-bb05-64055d460159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5 ± 0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191025}] :answer-field/id #uuid "55334543-c991-57be-8ebf-858dbe1aa42f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191020}] :question/id #uuid "96fdabc0-86d9-5d27-bd9c-2df7a7fc3ade" :question/math-academy-id "q-159567" :question/problem "Let $y^(^) = β^(^)_{0} + β^(^)_{1}x = 1.5 + 3.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_{1}, y_{1}), …, (x_{18}, y_{18})$ of $n = 18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 2.4$, the mean square error of the model is $2.53$, and $S_{xx} = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2} = 484$, find a $90 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.746) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $β^(^)_{0}$ is given by $SE[β^(^)_{0}] = \\sqrt{MSE \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))}$*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203752 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203987 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ac3dfe7-c2fe-555a-b595-1d190d05085f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability for a Sample Proportion"}] :question/id #uuid "f69fbd84-8d87-5e68-af93-345cdf64fd6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-15126" :question/problem "Let $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{30}$ be a random sample of size $n = 30$ conducted without replacement from a population of $N=100$ individuals where $25\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nFind the probability that the proportion of individuals in the sample having the characteristic is between $35\\%$ and $50\\%$. Express your answer in terms of $\\Phi(z)$, the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution." :question/worked-solution "Given a population of $N$ individuals in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nThe factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis the finite population correction factor for the variance. This is used in cases of finite samples where\n\n- $n\\gt 5\\%\\cdot N$ (i.e., the sample comprises more than $5\\%$ of the population), and\n- The sample is conducted without replacement.\n\nIf a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement*, then the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 30, \\qquad p = 0.25, \\qquad N = 100\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle n & \\displaystyle  > 5 \\mathrm{\\% } \\cdot  100 = 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle n p & \\displaystyle  = 30 ( 0.25 ) = 7.5 > 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle n ( 1 - p ) & \\displaystyle  = 30 ( 0.75 ) = 22.5 > 5\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nwe can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\np^(^) \\mid ∼N(p, (p(1 - p))/(n) \\cdot \\frac{N - n}{N - 1}); ∼N(0.25, (0.25(1 - 0.25))/(30) \\cdot \\frac{70}{99}); ∼N(0.25, \\frac{7}{1584})\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n\n$$\nP\\left(0.35 \\lt \\widehat{\\,p} \\lt 0.5\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0.35 < p^(^) < 0.5) &= P(\\frac{0.35 - 0.25}{\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{1584}}} < Z < \\frac{0.5 - 0.25}{\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{1584}}}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(1.50 < Z < 3.76) \\\\\n&= P(Z < 3.76) - P(Z < 1.50) \\\\\n&= Φ(3.76) - Φ(1.50)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP\\left(0.35 \\lt \\widehat{\\,p} \\lt 0.5\\right) \\approx \\Phi(3.76) - \\Phi(1.50)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203753 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203988 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f7586b-170c-5236-ae18-0ea6af5bc83c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability for a Sample Proportion in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "85d17e0c-373c-5ae7-b5a6-36e401ef4d0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-15127" :question/problem "Of the $182$ members of a gym, $80\\%$ work out for more than $100$ minutes each visit. Suppose we draw a random sample of $32$ members of this gym. Find the probability that the number of gym members in the sample who work out for more than $100$ minutes each visit is at least $24$.\n\n*Hint: The table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, -0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1711 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1685 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1660 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1635 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1977 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1949 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1922 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1894 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2266 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2236 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2206 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2177 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population of $N$ individuals in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\cdot \\dfrac{N-n}{N-1} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nThe factor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{N-n}{N-1}\n$$\n\nis the finite population correction factor for the variance. This is used in cases of finite samples where\n\n- $n\\gt 5\\%\\cdot N$ (i.e., the sample comprises more than $5\\%$ of the population), and\n- the sample is conducted without replacement.\n\nIf a sample is conducted from a finite population *with replacement*, then the sample data are independent, and a finite population correction factor is not required.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn = 32, \\qquad p = 0.8, \\qquad N = 182\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle n & \\displaystyle  > 5 \\mathrm{\\% } \\cdot  182 = 9.1 \\\\ \\displaystyle n p & \\displaystyle  = 32 ( 0.8 ) = 25.6 > 5 \\\\ \\displaystyle n ( 1 - p ) & \\displaystyle  = 32 ( 0.2 ) = 6.4 > 5\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nwe can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\np^(^) \\mid ∼N(p, (p(1 - p))/(n) \\cdot \\frac{N - n}{N - 1}); ∼N(0.8, (0.8(1 - 0.8))/(32) \\cdot \\frac{150}{181}); ∼N(0.8, \\frac{3}{724})\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n\n$$\nP\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} \\geq \\dfrac{24}{32}\\right) = P\\left(\\widehat{\\,p} \\geq 0.75 \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, we convert $\\widehat{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(p^(^) \\ge 0.75) &= P(Z \\ge \\frac{0.75 - 0.8}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{724}}}) \\\\\n&= P(Z \\ge - 0.78) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z < - 0.78) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(-0.78)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-0.78) = 0.2177\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(p^(^) \\ge 0.75) &= 1 - Φ(-0.78) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.2177 \\\\\n&= 0.7823\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203801 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203993 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38357a4e-20fd-5667-882d-3310fa832bd5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding MM Estimators for Continuous Uniform Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "77223e04-b4a3-5021-8d2d-9506d8ff0aa8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16381" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{5}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim U(\\theta, \\theta+6)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter and $\\theta>0$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, x_3\\ldots,x_{5}$ you're given that\n$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{5} x_i^2 = 140$.\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimator for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{140}{5}=28\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables follow a uniform distribution $U(a,b)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= (((θ + 6) - θ)^{2})/(12) + ((θ + (θ + 6))/(2))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{12} + (θ + 3)^{2} \\\\\n&= 3 + θ^{2} + 6θ + 9 \\\\\n&= θ^{2} + 6θ + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sample and theoretical second moments and using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 6θ^(^)_{MM} + 12 &= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 6θ^(^)_{MM} + 12 &= 28 \\\\\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 6θ^(^)_{MM} - 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(θ^(^)_{MM} + 8)(θ^(^)_{MM} - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= -8, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= 2\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since we are given that $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203810 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203990 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f65904e-df13-5e81-b138-3e7e839c6f33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding MM Estimators for Poisson Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "2a677d19-4b4c-526a-af9c-cb78a92ea88e" :question/math-academy-id "e-16438" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{12}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{12}$, you're given that\n$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i^2 = 360$.\n\nCalculate the method of moments estimate for $\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimate for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{360}{12}=30\n$$\n\nThe variables $X_i$ follow a Poisson distribution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\theta, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\theta\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= θ + θ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the sample and theoretical second moments, using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}^2 + \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = 30\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + θ^(^)_{MM} &= 30 \\\\\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + θ^(^)_{MM} - 30 &= 0 \\\\\n(θ^(^)_{MM} + 6)(θ^(^)_{MM} - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = -6, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = 5\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203811 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203991 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae651f06-2027-590f-8ba7-f77224f7ad0d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding MM Estimators for Geometric Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "1aa0966b-84d2-554e-bd7d-0d7b8deff82b" :question/math-academy-id "e-16439" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{15}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Geom}(\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{15}$, you're given that\n$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{15} x_i^2 = 30$.\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$. Round your final answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimate for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{30}{15} =2\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables $X_i$ follow a Geometric distribution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1}{\\theta}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1-\\theta}{\\theta^2}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - θ}{θ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{θ^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - θ}{θ^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the sample and theoretical second moments, using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2-\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}}{\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}^2} = 2\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 - θ^(^)_{MM})/(θ^(^)_{MM}^{2}) &= 2 \\\\\n2 - θ^(^)_{MM} &= 2θ^(^)_{MM}^{2} \\\\\n2θ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + θ^(^)_{MM} - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nθ^(^)_{MM} &= (-1 ± \\sqrt{1^{2} - 4(2)(-2)})/(2 \\cdot 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} \\approx 0.781, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} \\approx -1.281\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.781}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203813 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203994 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98e123fc-79b0-59af-b65f-1b9b79141f7c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding MM Estimators for Binomial Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "b20979c7-dca4-5e73-b171-12adc7b77852" :question/math-academy-id "e-16441" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_6$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i\\sim B(3, \\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2,\\ldots, x_{6}$, you're given that\n$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{6} x_i^2 = 10$.\n\nFind the method of moments estimate $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}$ for $\\theta$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimator for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{10}{6}=\\dfrac53\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables follow a binomial distribution $B(m,\\theta)$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = m\\theta, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = m\\theta (1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= mθ(1 - θ) + m^{2}θ^{2} \\\\\n&= 3θ(1 - θ) + 3^{2}θ^{2} \\\\\n&= 3θ - 3θ^{2} + 9θ^{2} \\\\\n&= 6θ^{2} + 3θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sample and theoretical second moments and using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6θ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 3θ^(^)_{MM} = \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n18θ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 9θ^(^)_{MM} - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^(^)_{MM} &= (-9 ± \\sqrt{9^{2} - 4(18)(-5)})/(2(18)) \\\\\nθ^(^)_{MM} &= \\frac{-3 ± 7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= -\\dfrac{5}{6}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\dfrac 13\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203814 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203995 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eef9a336-451a-5a59-a3c6-231dfd020c50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding MM Estimators for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "3fe4a424-0394-55e2-a316-97abf62aa416" :question/math-academy-id "e-16442" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{10}$ is an I.I.D. random sample with $X_i \\sim N(\\mu, \\, \\sigma^2)$, where $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are unknown parameters. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10}$, you're given that\n$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i = 70, \\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i^2 = 550$.\n\nFind the method of moments estimates $\\widehat{\\mu}_{MM}$ and $\\widehat{\\sigma^2}_{MM}$ for $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimator for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the values in the sample and the sum of squares. So, we can calculate the first and second sample moments:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{1} &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} = \\frac{70}{10} = 7 \\\\\nm_{2} &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} = \\frac{550}{10} = 55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables $X_i$ follow a normal distribution. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}] &= μ \\\\\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} = σ^{2} + μ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sample and theoretical first and second moments, using the estimators $\\widehat\\mu_{MM}$ and $\\widehat{\\sigma^2}_{MM}$ in place of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ^(^)_{MM} &= 7, \\\\\nσ^{2}^(^)_{MM} + μ^(^)_{MM}^{2} &= 55 \\\\\nσ^{2}^(^)_{MM} + 7^{2} &= 55 \\\\\nσ^{2}^(^)_{MM} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203986 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5511228-4de5-5aaa-96c1-18cb4222a179" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations Where a Correction Factor Is Necessary"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e890a8a2-8682-51bb-9489-d490973a7aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201076} {:answer/id #uuid "02a7841e-447f-536a-8672-96849b956dfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201077} {:answer/id #uuid "2cff1bc8-a29a-5545-936b-72fa0902d8c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201078} {:answer/id #uuid "4da0cb9e-258e-5eb6-9e78-06323110e143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201079} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbaddbb-e6f5-5eb6-b2ae-8ce5c43c90bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201080}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f463622d-79d0-5488-a841-2fe27df4cd14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201075}] :question/id #uuid "af34435d-dcd7-5ff0-8746-2acd4720c97f" :question/math-academy-id "q-225656" :question/problem "A bag contains $250$ red balls and $150$ white balls. We wish to construct a statistical model for $p^(^)$, the proportion of white balls in a random sample.\n\nConsider the following random samples.\n\n1. $30$ balls are drawn from the bag without replacement.\n2. $22$ balls are drawn from the bag with replacement.\n3. $15$ balls are drawn from the bag without replacement.\n\nWhich of these sampling methods requires a finite population correction factor to be applied to model $p^(^)$ as normal?\n\n*Hint: In all cases, the requirement $np > 5$ and $n(1 - p) > 5$ is satisfied.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204357 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203998 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12d8ef73-7e7a-58c0-8363-62d9be50bd6c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Differences in Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9d523ae-c7f1-5a38-a691-1ca90aa16ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.74 ± 0.063" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201193} {:answer/id #uuid "f88242dd-6329-5c9a-b465-aaef6f538626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.74 ± 0.221" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201194} {:answer/id #uuid "192f48f6-0ec1-52db-938d-52ff08d0d49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1 ± 0.090" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201195} {:answer/id #uuid "509d63be-d04c-5ad0-8ea3-0fb1e40bda23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1 ± 0.105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201196} {:answer/id #uuid "5271ff62-972d-5417-b658-3aede6ae0be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.1 ± 0.078" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201197}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d74d3ca6-46a1-5cde-8ecd-b8e7aef031e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186201192}] :question/id #uuid "e92d23a2-7d1b-59b8-a1ae-7f3ef91904b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-226122" :question/problem "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8d