17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f222beb-d0d3-5b6b-9403-a17b96ad359a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433234} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac0821a-b6aa-5317-a297-a9a3dc05a88c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433236} {:answer/id #uuid "6251ea59-705b-5847-9012-57b584fac2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433240} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab7f0a0-fe29-5760-8c40-d82548fb38aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433242} {:answer/id #uuid "a77cbf44-79a7-59ff-b259-6795d0da283d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a77cbf44-79a7-59ff-b259-6795d0da283d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433252} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42082b2-5441-549c-87bb-3c77d0e96111" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27aa799c-ba85-595b-949d-753bf0289dce" :question/math-academy-id "q-88953" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n-21,\\,19,\\,-15,\\,31\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-21+19-15+31) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{4} \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02adcd4a-5393-58b0-af0e-a5b18c91534f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433222} {:answer/id #uuid "34cc7ecf-2f4c-5241-9894-f5ea73dcf579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433232} {:answer/id #uuid "700c45ef-e183-58ae-8be4-2043b49b7309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433243} {:answer/id #uuid "98ff96fb-ce0e-5a70-b94c-3e947c6b6136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433249} {:answer/id #uuid "dc874edf-13f5-50cb-895a-98aa264eefce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "700c45ef-e183-58ae-8be4-2043b49b7309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433243} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6cab445-bef6-5be3-a473-b44ea983a3a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cf665ff-9d15-5f41-89fa-cbf6ac797c1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-149730" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n4,\\,19,\\,24,\\,3,\\,5,\\,20\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4+19+24+3+5+20) \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{6} \\\\  & =12.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f09cf15-103d-541e-82dd-b2f4e5370765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433224} {:answer/id #uuid "299a2542-20f0-5ec9-bb4f-fafa0112f246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433229} {:answer/id #uuid "55ab3159-d3b0-585a-8056-7b3695d96562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433238} {:answer/id #uuid "bc556c75-d056-5567-a1b4-76f0a0a4f482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433254} {:answer/id #uuid "fc778621-9543-50d5-9816-52c0237f415a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc778621-9543-50d5-9816-52c0237f415a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433263} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebb0bdf5-b5f8-53d7-a442-39faad4214bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9dd1476b-baa3-50d0-a7b4-7fe6d079ea16" :question/math-academy-id "q-88948" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n7,\\,-3,\\,-6,\\,9,\\,5\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(7-3-6+9+5) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461b536d-35a4-5eec-8ebc-2f8031802f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433235} {:answer/id #uuid "5a404743-c5cf-582c-a1ec-2225f086800a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433239} {:answer/id #uuid "67117110-e8f8-5b91-b3a3-8be85e5621a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433241} {:answer/id #uuid "d8554b31-1741-5f0d-a91c-59c30e331d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433256} {:answer/id #uuid "fec696b0-3d9d-5445-9e0f-ef42b510bd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67117110-e8f8-5b91-b3a3-8be85e5621a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433241} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2cc3650-c81d-58a7-9545-21babf198029" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c4de41-aa4b-5ba9-8cf4-45e87c95dba0" :question/math-academy-id "q-88945" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n23,\\,9,\\,19,\\,10,\\,15,\\,14\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(23+9+19+10+15+14) \\\\  & =\\frac{90}{6} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a29066f-c5c6-5f13-9704-332d81aafa1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433223} {:answer/id #uuid "1e63e961-b7cd-57a7-b177-22138ad08e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433227} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9c2705-b383-5552-8765-afa77e795806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433230} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1a5509-8a63-5798-9db0-2a0cde6f6ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433251} {:answer/id #uuid "ef41a907-369a-5ca3-9f40-ac473c2bc7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef41a907-369a-5ca3-9f40-ac473c2bc7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433262} :answer-field/id #uuid "89547a27-782d-5edb-a84a-c342d94aab57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fac2f103-d2fc-584f-ab2c-f1bc5b2aaa60" :question/math-academy-id "q-139803" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n-9,\\,-4,\\,1,\\,7,\\,-3,\\,6,\\,10,\\,12\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-9-4+1+7-3+6+10+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{8} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f582a26-8981-5aeb-8c9a-798c57cf7fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.08,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441034} {:answer/id #uuid "46e06021-ad9c-5a64-acca-08da3cae5944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.92,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441050} {:answer/id #uuid "94bc8e49-7700-5fe4-9f33-703b9393864d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.08,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441069} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c53a96-9533-5724-8502-b139f44c78a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.92,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0003" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441073} {:answer/id #uuid "d43f3441-cdd6-58be-bcdf-3e813693eab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.08,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f582a26-8981-5aeb-8c9a-798c57cf7fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.08,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441034} :answer-field/id #uuid "e48890ff-d63d-57c7-b77b-86bd5ac0b261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1d5c4439-72cc-5a29-8805-27f9db8f5589" :question/math-academy-id "q-146450" :question/problem "Suppose that  $8%$ of a particular university's student population attends the law school. A random sample of $n=100$ students is taken from the population and asked which school they attend.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of students in the sample who attend the law school? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=8%=0.08,$ and $n=100.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =8%=0.08, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.08(1-0.08)}{100} \\\\  & ≈0.0007\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59127361-a7c6-54be-8be7-6e1bedd5c145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.85,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441053} {:answer/id #uuid "9073635c-c291-5d2a-89b8-35dca7c94744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.005" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441068} {:answer/id #uuid "afbdf9af-161b-565e-b86c-9d06f52deaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441075} {:answer/id #uuid "de1a4b59-e152-53b9-b98f-578e556fb269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441088} {:answer/id #uuid "e4313ddb-7ae8-58f9-81a8-402669642b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.85,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1a4b59-e152-53b9-b98f-578e556fb269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.15,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.007" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441088} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ae3297d-2ab7-525a-83e9-568931f0c244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e62b0fb-05ae-5348-8fa8-26e1b9d8b974" :question/math-academy-id "q-146441" :question/problem "A bag contains a large number of equally-sized buttons, of which $15%$ are white. A random sample of $n=18$ buttons is taken from the bag.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of white buttons in the sample? Round your answers to $3$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of buttons in the bag is significantly larger than the number in the sample.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=15%=0.15,$ and $n=18.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =15%=0.15, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{18} \\\\  & ≈0.007\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c329a0-2b65-5553-80ed-a63f5cbd64ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.1,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441040} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfe0081-89a0-5f3a-8044-d6903a1aec34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.9,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441067} {:answer/id #uuid "b32e31b8-7ee2-5888-8fe2-b6582e214786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.1,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0081" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441076} {:answer/id #uuid "e86497ec-cf36-5810-9fc4-eb1d50286589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.9,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0081" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441092} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbe8668-1cdb-5f50-82eb-12abca7abfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.1,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbbe8668-1cdb-5f50-82eb-12abca7abfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.1,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441098} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14feaef-0c14-5d3c-a394-226d0415b9de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d736eeb-4230-5251-a2a5-38a3eb699594" :question/math-academy-id "q-146449" :question/problem "In a sports warehouse, a large container contains several thousand baseballs. According to a report from the manufacturer, $10%$ of these balls are defective. \n\nA random sample of size $n=20$ is taken from the container. What is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of defective balls in the sample?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of baseballs in the box is significantly larger than the number in the sample.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=10%=0.1,$ and $n=20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =10%=0.1, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.1(1-0.1)}{20} \\\\  & =0.0045.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed2ddc3-b4dc-59ae-9d8b-0318dc6b3088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.78,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0069" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441038} {:answer/id #uuid "25672540-7f15-59d4-a9f0-a76441476fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.78,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0081" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441039} {:answer/id #uuid "4fecbd1a-4f33-567e-8978-268629ae9bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.22,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0081" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441051} {:answer/id #uuid "83135bc2-29fc-5570-8ca1-f6392afa94d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.22,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0096" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441065} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57b051-ab02-5e79-99b8-56f8c9fadb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.22,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0069" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec57b051-ab02-5e79-99b8-56f8c9fadb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.22,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0069" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441093} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e53a0ea-2177-529a-b607-5f54cb66e41c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "548372c4-2d00-514c-b6a7-43bd98885c5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146447" :question/problem "Suppose that $22%$ of a particular population of teenagers like science fiction novels. A random sample of $n=25$ teenagers is taken from the population and asked about their favorite novel genres.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of teenagers in the sample who like science fiction novels? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=22%=0.22,$ and $n=25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =22%=0.22, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.22(1-0.22)}{25} \\\\  & ≈0.0069\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184208 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2aa1599e-b9c0-5282-ac58-b10211cb7d30" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15dde39d-88be-58d2-9cfe-766d0c016346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443415} {:answer/id #uuid "26f31e5b-1fa1-56d2-8786-d2c285ab5fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443418} {:answer/id #uuid "74e5d5fc-d551-5446-8f5a-277c7dc3e028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443435} {:answer/id #uuid "90b3402d-6afa-58de-87a4-ace03f1b7710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443438} {:answer/id #uuid "f3562734-54c8-5167-889c-5d3f14bb8dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90b3402d-6afa-58de-87a4-ace03f1b7710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443438} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1e9e1a-855b-5e0e-8730-feb174aced84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f7a0341-d787-5ede-9a89-8dbc8c2dee11" :question/math-academy-id "q-139856" :question/problem "A random sample of size $n=51$ is conducted from a population. It is known that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{51}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=24.5\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere each ${x}_{i}$ is a sample observation. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population standard deviation. Round your answer to two decimal places where appropriate." :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{51}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=24.5$ and $n=51.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{51-1}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}=0.49.$ We take the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\sigma }=\\sqrt{0.49}=0.7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443479 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184194 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "897ae010-a9a2-5971-a1ec-c2618cb8ad2c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Population Variance From Sample Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070b0ec1-e144-5414-93ef-c5d5462a2e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443412} {:answer/id #uuid "65e4a95b-42ab-5a2c-99dd-38bc188bce86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443432} {:answer/id #uuid "cadf2674-531a-5eb7-beb3-ef85205e6e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443450} {:answer/id #uuid "cf85e7e1-51e9-5c34-9e99-adf0e1b11997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443452} {:answer/id #uuid "f6abbcb5-083f-5afd-b9e1-d8bdcfede116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf85e7e1-51e9-5c34-9e99-adf0e1b11997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443452} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7c916ff-d433-5b36-a6c0-190697c1b089" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443476}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f7daca4-074f-50e6-8f6a-0126ae82d27d" :question/math-academy-id "q-89000" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an unbiased estimate of the population variance.\n\n$$\n19,\\,11,\\,20,\\,14,\\,16\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(19+11+20+14+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{5} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({19}^{2}+{11}^{2}+{20}^{2}+{14}^{2}+{16}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,334}{5} \\\\  & =266.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =266.8-{16}^{2} \\\\  & =10.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 10.8 \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{4} \\\\  & =13.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $13.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184195 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb182688-828c-5252-8d6d-d09c93ba2bc8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Estimate of the Population Variance Using the Alternative Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4782e79d-2978-56f4-8596-d71632fec800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443421} {:answer/id #uuid "6efb4a9f-ee23-54d8-bb63-67d67da3a792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443434} {:answer/id #uuid "c64059bc-60a7-5cf6-8990-bc4a4acd021d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443448} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e83c13-78d9-509c-937f-6d79daf8d735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443453} {:answer/id #uuid "d2628084-0fcd-5c98-90c0-5d2f55fb9f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921