set{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes the unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nFirst, we will compute the quantity\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- The mean of the data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{5}(13+15+9+11+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{5} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The mean of the squared data is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{5}({13}^{2}+{15}^{2}+{9}^{2}+{11}^{2}+{12}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{740}{5} \\\\  & =148.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} & =148-{12}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5-1}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71ec1015-67ae-532c-90ae-ecdaface5f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.4,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485960} {:answer/id #uuid "a2dbf744-dd49-5c6d-a61b-984a7259504c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486057} {:answer/id #uuid "adf531a6-7319-5b80-9274-0509621d73e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486077} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4631fc-533d-5b33-96dd-c7fb0f7aac14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.4,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486133} {:answer/id #uuid "fd23b3bb-07c4-5f49-97bc-5ab8e4e8d978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd23b3bb-07c4-5f49-97bc-5ab8e4e8d978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.6,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.012" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486227} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6c7b60b-ebc0-552f-bb22-591e7a44c23f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1024622-b9b9-5914-883d-61f4135d4db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-146442" :question/problem "Suppose that $60%$ of the child population enrolled in a baseball academy is right-handed. A random sample of $n=20$ children is taken from the population and asked about their dominant hand.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of right-handed children in the sample? \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=60%=0.6,$ and $n=20.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =60%=0.6, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{20} \\\\  & =0.012.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019c2c01-30dd-5c5f-8b90-73f9344dc881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.25,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485766} {:answer/id #uuid "02fb1520-a9c6-5a73-a112-44948c9ae426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.25,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485769} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc72d5e-57f2-5ca3-8e38-bf9dd994405d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.75,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485819} {:answer/id #uuid "37521a95-ddd5-5ea7-b2b3-b4bdd56c6670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.25,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485870} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a5fb5b-749a-576d-91ab-0632592d5e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.75,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "019c2c01-30dd-5c5f-8b90-73f9344dc881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.25,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0075" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485766} :answer-field/id #uuid "af58c111-f99a-57f4-a10e-1136008a2d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5101e52-f007-51fe-8d87-cde53f7bb6a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-146444" :question/problem "Suppose that $25%$ of a high school student population like mathematics. A random sample of $n=25$ high school students is taken from the population and asked about their favorite subject.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of students in the sample who like mathematics? \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=25%=0.25,$ and $n=25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =25%=0.25, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{25} \\\\  & =0.0075.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519d160c-0ea4-58a9-a947-73fc7f0938a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.05,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485913} {:answer/id #uuid "8a689ad1-9962-554c-8093-e6837ab2abcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.95,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486011} {:answer/id #uuid "c24a5121-16cd-59c2-9c8d-82042ff6cfe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.95,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486113} {:answer/id #uuid "d000e6d6-4f1c-5d12-881f-a9853df1ad21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.05,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0049" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486139} {:answer/id #uuid "d10e4dfb-9f2d-5ff6-9adf-833a9cdd919b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.05,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0064" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "519d160c-0ea4-58a9-a947-73fc7f0938a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.05,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.0032" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485913} :answer-field/id #uuid "5432d2f4-4b49-5e55-a1cc-c6c715583c98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fff98227-c5ba-54d3-b84c-be9e152eefa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-146451" :question/problem "It is known that  $5%$ of the researchers in a particular university work in neurobiology. A random sample of $n=15$ researchers is taken from the population and asked about their work area.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of researchers in the sample working in neurobiology? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=5%=0.05,$ and $n=15.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =5%=0.05, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.05(1-0.05)}{15} \\\\  & ≈0.0032\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2ffbca-0cac-5940-a7dc-6a3aa9f30b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6954" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501733} {:answer/id #uuid "b739cc9f-d7c6-57b4-96f4-f38acc56c93f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7816" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501831} {:answer/id #uuid "d46951e0-79b5-5171-aac4-104568cfcf6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7924" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501887} {:answer/id #uuid "d9b614cd-f118-5752-a14a-3e03c66ef5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501900} {:answer/id #uuid "e41e3f60-af2a-5d03-ae51-9b94e91c8682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9723" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9b614cd-f118-5752-a14a-3e03c66ef5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501900} :answer-field/id #uuid "603f3c7e-5a6d-59bf-b0ec-1681329ffe35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11e7255b-c3e0-5351-a566-b77404e961ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-147071" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=99$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=99,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=99(0.45)=44.55>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=99(1-0.45)=54.45>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=99$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{99}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0025).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0025}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.00) \\\\  & =Φ(1.00).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.00) & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5) & =Φ(0.8413) \\\\  & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e4ff370-88d6-5364-ac58-0e5d98900a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501519} {:answer/id #uuid "9a40a784-700c-5e4c-9e97-c0835f0a60b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501757} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bf5c83-f1eb-519c-8394-47157850924b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501781} {:answer/id #uuid "d48cb210-f5a6-59f2-923a-069fc7f25fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501888} {:answer/id #uuid "f616020e-49fd-5757-a8e6-0c21672f2fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d48cb210-f5a6-59f2-923a-069fc7f25fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501888} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d798ec-e079-52f8-a343-6ba9d9d2c646" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19ebeffd-a45a-5a50-925e-63646f4bc31a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146809" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=52$ from a population of size $N=120$ in which $8%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=4$ from a population of size $N=200$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=20$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =52, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & <52.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =4 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10\\,✓ \\\\ np & =4(0.7) \\\\  & =2.8 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =20 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25\\,✓ \\\\ np & =20(0.70) \\\\  & =14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20(1-0.70) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91695da2-de95-5431-872f-19b52844bdd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.53)-Φ(-1.86)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501743} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c38604-6309-551b-ac28-d167af1123ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.23)-Φ(-2.96)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501772} {:answer/id #uuid "b13dcd98-7237-5fd6-a1e6-940842cbdb82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.66)-Φ(-2.23)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501820} {:answer/id #uuid "be8b84ad-a507-55af-aa58-6bcf6faf6c0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.55)-Φ(-3.14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501849} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d26bd1-f8e6-5285-9981-178e6521065c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8d26bd1-f8e6-5285-9981-178e6521065c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501960} :answer-field/id #uuid "8db11675-d772-5e72-8d9a-3a6e110d5729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2735f324-7321-5142-98fc-d20d8884d660" :question/math-academy-id "q-147504" :question/problem "It is known that $35%$ of the people arriving at a clothing store buy something. If the manager randomly samples $65$ customers, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ of them bought something. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=65,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.35.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=65(0.35)=22.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=65(1-0.35)=42.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=65$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,\\frac{0.35(1-0.35)}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ people bought something, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{65}<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <\\frac{26}{65} \\\\ 0.2<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =P(\\frac{0.2-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}<Z<\\frac{0.4-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.54<Z<0.85) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.85)-P(Z<-2.54) \\\\  & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c8f58f-95c5-542e-9b35-85744163dc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5172" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501535} {:answer/id #uuid "a0fa6a07-689e-56bd-934e-366f80119f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2517" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501770} {:answer/id #uuid "c35ea702-ea78-5e82-b0cb-6e3428dbb539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2157" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501856} {:answer/id #uuid "c8967056-a8d1-5db9-a7a2-0afcb5f80f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1752" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501864} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d33bab-f28f-555d-b84b-cc1cf9685d42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2715" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35ea702-ea78-5e82-b0cb-6e3428dbb539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2157" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b58d986a-b956-51c7-a441-3edd459a5e37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6c90ea0b-854a-5f39-a73d-92e856698eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-147212" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=125$ from a population where $75%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $65%$ but less than $72%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2266\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2236\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2206\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2578\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2912\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2843\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0051\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0049\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0069\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0068\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0066\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0091\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0089\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0087\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.75.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.75)=93.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.75)=31.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,\\frac{0.75(1-0.75)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.72).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.72) & =P(\\frac{0.65-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.72-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.58<Z\\le -0.77) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.77)-P(Z\\le -2.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.77) & =0.2206 \\\\ Φ(-2.58) & =0.0049.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.72) & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58) \\\\  & =0.2206-0.0049 \\\\  & =0.2157.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd46750-aab1-5ab4-8f35-4bb7abe41d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501490} {:answer/id #uuid "508c1697-4000-5926-99b2-497ea4d6c810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501612} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad952b8-2ed2-584a-967d-7f752886a23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501664} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c31986-9c09-50b3-be41-6a9593a36e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501892} {:answer/id #uuid "e34c4d92-333e-5b82-80d3-ad763eae7cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ad952b8-2ed2-584a-967d-7f752886a23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501664} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa3a4acb-3217-5494-99eb-6860b8a21200" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75b5d8b1-9316-5672-a007-ededd1581f02" :question/math-academy-id "q-146819" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=200$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $80%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=30$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =200 \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & <200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.8) \\\\  & =32 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.8) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =30(0.7) \\\\  & =21 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =30(1-0.7) \\\\  & =9 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07440603-54b5-5976-899e-ae9b9661d017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.81)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501483} {:answer/id #uuid "32730632-54f9-51bd-9b77-5eb922390acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.47)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501559} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8d336e-ed5a-5688-ade1-9317c97d93ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.97)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501610} {:answer/id #uuid "e4626d23-d099-557f-8b2c-0a74d5d57629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2.11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501926} {:answer/id #uuid "f139c347-a6cc-572a-946f-3b855b388e8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.65)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07440603-54b5-5976-899e-ae9b9661d017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.81)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501483} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c885ba5-a648-5bca-b5b2-398b1f373335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a5fcfb6-efc6-55da-8a4d-4dde0bab77e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147448" :question/problem "An amusement park knows that $25%$ of its visitors ride the rollercoaster. The manager of the park conducts a random sample of $125$ visitors. Find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $40$ of them rode the rollercoaster. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.25.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.25)=31.25>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.25)=93.75>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $40$ visitors in the sample rode the rollercoaster, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{40}{125}=0.32.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.32).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.32-0.25}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.81).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56867d82-3d07-5414-9450-8f0c79240546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.78)-Φ(-0.78)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501624} {:answer/id #uuid "68727b43-f29b-53a1-a5d7-6dcd49a3a034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.36)-Φ(-0.36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501657} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dbdab0-ba6b-5637-9d3a-42a4f9671754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.96)-Φ(-0.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501782} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9cff08-4800-5f04-8bf4-570544c083f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.80)-Φ(-0.80)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501801} {:answer/id #uuid "fc19f28c-42f1-5740-9078-6e1e7a33e836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc19f28c-42f1-5740-9078-6e1e7a33e836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501968} :answer-field/id #uuid "3db1ac1a-fba9-53f0-abaa-ce90a089ca8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84cf65c5-831d-5253-bb62-b35c4c8f232c" :question/math-academy-id "q-146942" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population where $20%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $15%$ and $25%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.2.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.2)=10>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.2)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,0.0032).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.15\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.25).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.15\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.25) & =P(\\frac{0.15-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.25-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.88\\le Z\\le 0.88) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.88)-P(Z\\le -0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3009884b-eaac-58c4-9dd7-476278e483b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2626" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501550} {:answer/id #uuid "43b7b2e8-0dd0-53f5-a8d4-5c5d905e9b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501590} {:answer/id #uuid "88a07982-8bca-5d34-ba03-ad213b50a5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0808" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501728} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ecc379-ae54-5c46-b4ed-94535e2a2e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501788} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d11df6-7dc5-50f2-bbd9-0d41650103a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43b7b2e8-0dd0-53f5-a8d4-5c5d905e9b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501590} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa9ea89e-2b67-50ed-9875-e233798706d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0e2a9c0-d660-51ad-8aa9-baeec300d0b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-146975" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=76$ from a population where $24%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $30%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=76,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.24.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=76(0.24)=18.24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=76(1-0.24)=57.76>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=76$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,\\frac{0.24(1-0.24)}{76}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.3-0.24}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.22) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.22).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.22) & =0.8888.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3) & =1-Φ(1.22) \\\\  & =1-0.8888 \\\\  & =0.1112.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4116f3f0-c977-5e04-8b37-cfee23a31d33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501586} {:answer/id #uuid "56fb8155-120b-5170-9145-1fd130c2ec2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501627} {:answer/id #uuid "619c4398-31f6-54ae-99e8-d78d34165a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501648} {:answer/id #uuid "ac32c0bc-5323-5faa-b8d5-33267bce243a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501808} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ba3ea6-389e-54cf-a4c9-3ac81a2a28ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56fb8155-120b-5170-9145-1fd130c2ec2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501627} :answer-field/id #uuid "e00782d6-c22d-5c54-843e-daefb08e60d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab0bcefd-4d59-56aa-b8ec-f3d0b1dc4757" :question/math-academy-id "q-146838" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ recently caught shrimps, where $55%$ of the shrimps in the sea weigh more than $10\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nII. A sample of $27$ companies, where $82%$ of the companies in the country employ more than $100$ people.\n\nIII. A sample of $100$ songs in a streaming service, given that $92%$ of the songs last less than $3$ minutes.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.55) \\\\  & =27.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.55) \\\\  & =22.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =27(0.82) \\\\  & =22.14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =27(1-0.82) \\\\  & =4.86 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.92) \\\\  & =92 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.92) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03359e21-7a1b-5dec-803d-c1cd7423ca1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5329" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501470} {:answer/id #uuid "45e1015c-d45f-5639-97c2-7d9314f8ad2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4275" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501593} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb8dfe6-123f-5cfc-a78b-fa1793a96281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4884" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501703} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4a2dca-96f1-5a35-91e8-35bd1ef6be15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2687" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501846} {:answer/id #uuid "e120a658-b96e-5c48-8cf4-5d70b8be76ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3954" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eb8dfe6-123f-5cfc-a78b-fa1793a96281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4884" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501703} :answer-field/id #uuid "f514e27c-296f-5be6-8d4d-2642d4f0a3fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c30b7d81-9bb5-5e5f-938c-eb285e73259b" :question/math-academy-id "q-147482" :question/problem "A smartphone app has $500\\,000$ users, $150\\,000$ of which have a paid account. If an app developer randomly samples $300$ users, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $72$ and $90$ (inclusive) users in the sample have a paid account. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0091\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0089\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0087\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0119\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0116\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0113\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0154\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0150\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0146\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5040\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5080\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5398\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5438\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5478\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=300,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{150\\,000}{500\\,000}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=300(0.3)=90>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=300(1-0.3)=210>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=300$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{300}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0007).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $72$ and $90$ of users in the sample have a paid account, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{72}{300}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{90}{300} \\\\ 0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =P(\\frac{0.24-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.3-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.27\\le Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0)=0.5000,\\,Φ(-2.27)=0.0116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27) \\\\  & =0.5000-0.0116 \\\\  & =0.4884.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502151 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f6ee384-815c-53b4-9f2d-c31907cf3c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501548} {:answer/id #uuid "4c291cdc-8fdf-5b18-b158-8a4495491a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501604} {:answer/id #uuid "82d21d0a-8fa8-5493-aa93-34dbcc67ac2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501716} {:answer/id #uuid "d97cc3ab-896e-5f3e-bae3-64970cbcd952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501899} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a565c8-6e99-5075-9f5d-a43101206bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c291cdc-8fdf-5b18-b158-8a4495491a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501604} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a7338c4-7992-59c5-b27c-44f71458a952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6efb51f-1cc6-56d3-90b2-77b3cfdb82a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-146816" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=100$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=60$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =100, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & <100.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =60 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =60(0.90) \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.90) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ff494bd-0b9f-5135-9d18-50e3d2df3439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.71)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501549} {:answer/id #uuid "50ba5d47-3502-5e34-8efb-9b1500551a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.79)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501613} {:answer/id #uuid "729c9ecc-6c95-5e5f-ad08-f8c44fba093f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.93)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501676} {:answer/id #uuid "8b101809-2ccd-5ced-a664-5deefedab9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501732} {:answer/id #uuid "9dca0f89-0c53-5160-acd7-38acb63d1d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.87)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dca0f89-0c53-5160-acd7-38acb63d1d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.87)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501763} :answer-field/id #uuid "b081ec7e-333d-5dd4-a804-4e114c60c34a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d98ad49e-5d4d-5c1b-b380-745357c60a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-146972" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=75,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=75(0.45)=33.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=75(1-0.45)=41.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=75$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0033).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.5) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0033}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 0.87) \\\\  & =Φ(0.87)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2443380b-5fb7-5644-9da4-9247216c51c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-2.45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501531} {:answer/id #uuid "250e3fc3-8889-5bac-9379-1c96182f3fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-1.62)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501534} {:answer/id #uuid "47f4e987-a1cd-511c-b8f3-f4bb08cf984c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-1.85)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501599} {:answer/id #uuid "7f968a03-d0e7-595f-852d-c5d8937237d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-2.25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501709} {:answer/id #uuid "d26bcec2-8907-5cfb-9e9c-5911985e9883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-2.78)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2443380b-5fb7-5644-9da4-9247216c51c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-2.45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501531} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3a76ecc-0207-5bbe-bd7d-f25da682e7e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fff0efc8-e9b9-52d5-b287-771e1cbfdc28" :question/math-academy-id "q-147479" :question/problem "A dried apricot producer knows that $70%$ of apricots weigh more than $40$ g. If the factory randomly samples $350$ apricots, find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $224$ apricots weigh more than $40$ g. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=350,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=350(0.7)=245>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=350(1-0.7)=105>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=350$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{350}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.0006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $224$ apricots in the sample weigh more than $40$ g, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{224}{350}=0.64.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.64-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.0006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-2.45) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.45).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d110045-4b65-57fd-8bdd-f29c56b9354b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.62\\pm 0.053" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505127} {:answer/id #uuid "16c22bf4-1ccd-5590-a353-2f8aba595ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.63\\pm 0.043" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505146} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfd3b35-f9d2-5dda-a6ab-2418f9a188a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.86\\pm 0.043" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505289} {:answer/id #uuid "6739d8fe-a3b3-5967-81be-436c26b89433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68\\pm 0.085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505312} {:answer/id #uuid "bdadb00d-76dd-5c2f-a3d9-6b6947e16f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68\\pm 0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdadb00d-76dd-5c2f-a3d9-6b6947e16f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.68\\pm 0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505473} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c49a0d1-81cf-5551-b8ab-2f04901b402c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "139a4b78-66bf-579e-be6b-e3d4e7bd7403" :question/math-academy-id "q-143319" :question/problem "A survey of $1\\,200$ randomly selected U.S. households reveals that $816$ of them own at least one pet. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one pet.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one pet, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\,200,$ and\n\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one pet can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{816}{1\\,200}=0.68\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=1\\,200\\cdot 0.68=816>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=1\\,200\\cdot (1-0.68)=384>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,68\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.68(1-0.68)}{1\\,200}} \\\\  & 0.68\\pm 0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ecc0a7-c18b-5d04-8b82-55c9c806c090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.52\\pm 0.380" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505106} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb55c69-b1d6-5bba-b48a-dfa81c0a824e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.23\\pm 0.208" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505212} {:answer/id #uuid "420d7c1a-501f-56be-927d-99c9e5d936d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.803" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505233} {:answer/id #uuid "9154af0c-0d34-5d22-8cc5-0fbe90b92aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505385} {:answer/id #uuid "c601ed4b-5156-562f-8160-e837ab35aea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.031" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9154af0c-0d34-5d22-8cc5-0fbe90b92aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505385} :answer-field/id #uuid "04ab939d-eb1c-5644-9dfb-10535344778d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55f340e1-5420-5652-bd4b-d4b5bdb68c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143281" :question/problem "The financial manager of a large company conducts a random sample of $120$ accounts receivable and finds that $42$ of them are more than $30$ days past due. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all of the company's accounts receivable that are more than $30$ days past due. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all accounts receivable from a certain company,\n\n- some of the accounts selected are more than $30$ days past due, and some are not,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=120,$ and\n\n- the proportion of accounts receivable in the sample that are more than $30$ days past due can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{42}{120}=0.35\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.35=42>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.35)=78>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.35\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.35(1-0.35)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.35\\pm 0.112\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d4a80f-7422-50eb-a2d2-982d8ab608b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\pm 0.051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505112} {:answer/id #uuid "241b9168-9165-5486-a2bb-6a5b1833da28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13\\pm 0.551" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505165} {:answer/id #uuid "74bffb6d-6a48-5bce-aa53-0c42cab5e2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\pm 0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505341} {:answer/id #uuid "9df41cfb-274a-5f20-b768-014759fce6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13\\pm 0.515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505414} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0ae98a-0528-502d-83ac-663a3b219c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\pm 0.505" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03d4a80f-7422-50eb-a2d2-982d8ab608b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3\\pm 0.051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505112} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9ab8b3f-5403-5ce1-916c-6c3e8a70acc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5b158e8c-e5d9-51ed-a647-72626d39b20a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143307" :question/problem "A survey shows that $66$ out of a random sample of $220$ fans of a popular MLB baseball team are against the way their current coach has run the team.\nFind a $90%$ confidence interval for the proportion of the team's fans who are against the way the coach has run the team.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of fans of a certain MLB baseball team,\n\n- some of the fans are against the way their current coach has run the team, and some are not,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=220,$ and\n\n- the proportion of fans from the sample who are against the way the coach has run the team can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{66}{220}=0.3\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=220\\cdot 0.3=66>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=220\\cdot (1-0.3)=154>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2e/2ed94fdbb0eb4cc1dda3720592488546a02e157598e77a70fbd02d916906b56f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{220}} \\\\  & 0.3\\pm 0.051\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505798 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f4f3b82-a533-5794-8ca9-4f94400ca4bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505330} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9841ca-0e3c-51de-9459-0e80399403b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505479} {:answer/id #uuid "f803f0e2-f22e-5648-9673-d0d814ff166b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.086" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505587} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ed2da9-ae01-5f78-8c2d-a19c608ee3a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.091" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505590} {:answer/id #uuid "fe71e4df-2883-5aae-aa32-c5d249040050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf9841ca-0e3c-51de-9459-0e80399403b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505479} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c12850-6cfd-5d6c-804a-41b990dd6bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acfb542c-f97d-5ef1-8816-235f54148d5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-225614" :question/problem "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=90$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.6,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=90$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.6,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=90\\cdot 0.6=54>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=90\\cdot (1-0.6)=36>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.6\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{90}} \\\\  & 0.6\\pm 0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a888794-f66d-5afe-bc58-23ba40e1a714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85\\pm 0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505125} {:answer/id #uuid "1b076401-8233-5f64-8e82-e1f1f914afe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85\\pm 0.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505152} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2c9957-7528-5f67-967a-1b97828bc052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15\\pm 0.078" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505221} {:answer/id #uuid "7d96a42b-49e1-507d-80f3-c1f792ef9a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15\\pm 0.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505356} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a6dcfc-fbd2-5ec7-b123-a5534cf1d684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15\\pm 0.059" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d96a42b-49e1-507d-80f3-c1f792ef9a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15\\pm 0.050" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505356} :answer-field/id #uuid "0699f92d-9412-559f-a1e2-75949a0088e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af2f2f44-7a26-5871-833f-ef0bfbcbad94" :question/math-academy-id "q-143374" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=140$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $15%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=140$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=15%=0.15,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=140\\cdot 0.15=21>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=140\\cdot (1-0.15)=119>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{140}} \\\\  & 0.15\\pm 0.050\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505831 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4686a3da-8b92-5b7e-88d8-f689c1ced722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.057" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505250} {:answer/id #uuid "c525d632-4b9e-57b5-a500-ffa007743cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505486} {:answer/id #uuid "d375fed6-79ea-53af-816c-a4fa67ee017e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505512} {:answer/id #uuid "f6539426-1de9-5b1b-9e35-db0b7f08102d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.067" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505583} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2f4a63-cf19-57eb-86ff-e3b5b84510a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.088" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa2f4a63-cf19-57eb-86ff-e3b5b84510a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.088" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505594} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a0c197-f408-509a-9c1f-a1eeb5f17467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6190115-0559-574a-ba01-3e43de706837" :question/math-academy-id "q-225615" :question/problem "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=120$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.4=48>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.4)=72>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.088\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1396af2f-0e9b-507e-82c5-413a18a2648c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.014" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505140} {:answer/id #uuid "968a4e50-5eb2-5a76-9933-3d7484528b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505393} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3b9c13-ba57-5851-a89d-22737e7176b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505408} {:answer/id #uuid "be9971c7-2bc3-51c0-88a9-04a0c458bf5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4\\pm 0.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505474} {:answer/id #uuid "f776c8d5-3609-5b90-9293-e091193acac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b3b9c13-ba57-5851-a89d-22737e7176b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.041" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505408} :answer-field/id #uuid "1704a036-d71b-5ebc-aa12-189bf93207bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ec6576c2-76eb-5af7-a883-a898d0a92018" :question/math-academy-id "q-143313" :question/problem "A survey of $480$ randomly selected U.S. adults reveals that $336$ of the respondents have read at least one book in the past year. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. adults who read at least one book in the past year.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of  all U.S. adults,\n\n- some of the respondents read at least one book in the past year, and some didn't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=480,$ and\n\n- the proportion of adults who read at least one book in the past year can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{336}{480}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=480\\cdot 0.7=336>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=480\\cdot (1-0.7)=144>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{480}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.041\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "254f74b7-918f-5b54-ab34-7dfd44a5c967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505168} {:answer/id #uuid "410ef78d-c017-5576-9592-1c5aedadc3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8\\pm 0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505231} {:answer/id #uuid "6fbc7a76-06b8-5674-a514-4c81e882c29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505333} {:answer/id #uuid "a2295786-3596-52cf-9fef-a42aad614fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8\\pm 0.072" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505427} {:answer/id #uuid "ef863121-834a-5b79-bbdd-ab9220c8ef49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.085" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fbc7a76-06b8-5674-a514-4c81e882c29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.060" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505333} :answer-field/id #uuid "94296483-dea2-5dbb-8a1c-46540a814eae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd266226-8e47-5093-b6f1-e853e4a5f194" :question/math-academy-id "q-143371" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=120$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.2,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.2=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.2)=96>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.2\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.2\\pm 0.060\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ae5602-2088-5fc5-8cd0-18b6e2828415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8117" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512113} {:answer/id #uuid "1073d13f-794e-5f37-a58c-9a508bde8a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7582" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512134} {:answer/id #uuid "27f8649f-e3ed-5db7-be91-6fb5aec1a2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9517" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512187} {:answer/id #uuid "473980d8-f328-531a-b6ba-ec572af6ec3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6299" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512248} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc9c121-5f5f-5fac-9860-8c845e4d48bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9352" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "473980d8-f328-531a-b6ba-ec572af6ec3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6299" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512248} :answer-field/id #uuid "42b08898-7743-5ffd-b5a7-2f858adb5713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "102d618f-6dbe-5b03-8427-52afbec9d15d" :question/math-academy-id "q-147211" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $70%$ but less than $80%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9599\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9608\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9671\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9678\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9686\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9738\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9744\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9750\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3300\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3669\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3632\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4013\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3974\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.72.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.72)=69.12>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.72)=26.88>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,\\frac{0.72(1-0.72)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,0.0021).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.7-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.44<Z\\le 1.75) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.75)-P(Z\\le -0.44) \\\\  & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.75) & =0.9599 \\\\ Φ(-0.44) & =0.3300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44) \\\\  & =0.9599-0.3300 \\\\  & =0.6299.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ae5c47-ac36-5863-b19b-b928b3e368a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0455" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512247} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8c7c87-239c-57d6-a57d-5db3fb45492c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512261} {:answer/id #uuid "5f83bd20-bc13-5e22-81e1-73b889147394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1235" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512327} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff81e55-fb61-53d9-a0ed-ee5226f18b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512400} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c2ca1f-4a64-547f-8f50-f67ee8173783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0735" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46ae5c47-ac36-5863-b19b-b928b3e368a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0455" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512247} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f23f906-2914-521c-94e2-15d11518c774" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7b25e27-3f05-5502-a400-9d84bc6b903e" :question/math-academy-id "q-147386" :question/problem "A fitness center has $1600$ members, $480$ of which play tennis. If the center randomly samples $60$ members, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9418\\,$ | $\\,0.9429\\,$ | $\\,0.9441\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ | $\\,0.9535\\,$ | $\\,0.9545\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ | $\\,0.9625\\,$ | $\\,0.9633\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=60,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{480}{1600}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=60(0.3)=18>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=60(1-0.3)=42>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=60$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & >\\frac{24}{60}=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.69)=0.9545.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}) & =1-Φ(1.69) \\\\  & =0.0455.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05de7114-c6d2-5aa0-af39-01c4eb3c61be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512117} {:answer/id #uuid "71142195-0b10-524d-8d89-cc3312aea868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.29)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512368} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfe4040-0a28-5e2a-beae-ec384a40584e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.78)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512429} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d038d4-8c45-51ea-a946-61383d3a330e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.93)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512562} {:answer/id #uuid "e08790cd-6ba8-55b1-8612-1e73f81cd371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05de7114-c6d2-5aa0-af39-01c4eb3c61be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.51)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512117} :answer-field/id #uuid "99799375-0cad-5fd7-aafe-9d47d5e34722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba83e244-060a-5dec-904e-55d3bc3fab0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146974" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=88$ from a population where $44%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $52%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=88,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.44.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=88(0.44)=38.72>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=88(1-0.44)=49.28>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=88$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,\\frac{0.44(1-0.44)}{88}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,0.0028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.52-0.44}{\\sqrt{0.0028}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.51) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.51) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fce1eb7-6882-55e9-9a78-19b8b9ec12de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516439} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c1dd4-4d83-5f50-ab22-450ede146cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0428" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516443} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ee7691-eaaa-5a44-8e4b-9c9a62cb1212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516455} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf18dde-67fe-5d37-ba3e-bc04e8232dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0784" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516579} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0516" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0516" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516615} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4643a15-31bb-5228-8c72-0d5dcdaebd79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08394bc0-c2d2-5c3c-828d-b5a2969449ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-147070" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $36%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $28%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0655\\,$ | $\\,0.0643\\,$ | $\\,0.0630\\,$ | $\\,0.0618\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ | $\\,0.0764\\,$ | $\\,0.0749\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.36.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.36)=34.56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.36)=61.44>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,\\frac{0.36(1-0.36)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.28-0.36}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.63) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.63) & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28) & =Φ(-1.63) \\\\  & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06171ae9-aab5-5555-8742-22098a666ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.19)-Φ(-1.19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516124} {:answer/id #uuid "076508d1-5160-580c-894f-c6ee9485bf2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.69)-Φ(-0.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516127} {:answer/id #uuid "54ade330-5c85-51a0-9b3d-21d097f4a0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2.26)-Φ(-2.26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516290} {:answer/id #uuid "551a8f15-eac2-577e-8054-fb57a38b76a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.36)-Φ(-1.36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516292} {:answer/id #uuid "f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516631} :answer-field/id #uuid "e66048ec-88b7-5ae4-8b5d-5521b6f747e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9faef3f5-2412-5ae3-81dd-9f520c533b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-146937" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $60%$ and $80%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.7)=56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.7)=24>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.002\\,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.6-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.95\\le Z\\le 1.95) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.95)-P(Z\\le -1.95) \\\\  & =Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0059189e-a1d5-5009-984c-ab56aaf9c32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.084" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523250} {:answer/id #uuid "06a23d11-10d2-51ff-b549-c564d4d0adc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523269} {:answer/id #uuid "91e6c7ed-6456-52e2-b6b3-ca05ed0e1fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523561} {:answer/id #uuid "cff2a8a8-d184-58b6-9e13-e0ce109be435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.021" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523692} {:answer/id #uuid "fbec8e4e-7d76-5bea-a85e-7474bdb2c704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06a23d11-10d2-51ff-b549-c564d4d0adc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.042" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523269} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ab0739-3e4c-54dd-b8d9-d593dca45aa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42df1748-d6ee-5969-968b-973a27a68070" :question/math-academy-id "q-143308" :question/problem "A poll of $800$ randomly selected U.S. Internet subscribers reveals that only $560$ of those interviewed are satisfied with their current internet provider. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. internet users that are satisfied with their current internet providers.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. internet users,\n\n- some internet subscribers are satisfied with their current internet provider, and some aren't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=800,$ and\n\n- the proportion of Internet subscribers from the sample who are satisfied with their current internet provider is found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{560}{800}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=800\\cdot 0.7=560>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=800\\cdot (1-0.7)=240>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{800}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "245260ce-f9eb-5103-abac-929724075c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523326} {:answer/id #uuid "341d3433-7189-5f66-87a1-9332a008905d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523363} {:answer/id #uuid "51816d49-6771-5f06-881f-567d136e9342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523418} {:answer/id #uuid "b891f11a-a92a-540d-8453-b59eaffc3349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.034" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523640} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5679a7-92b1-5e2b-b3f0-4824eb166850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51816d49-6771-5f06-881f-567d136e9342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523418} :answer-field/id #uuid "acae107c-a0bc-5d9a-b751-b05730ad5a81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "53bd4dd2-6540-514d-83fb-ebaccad00681" :question/math-academy-id "q-143381" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=160$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $5%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=160$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=5%=0.05,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=160\\cdot 0.05=8>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=160\\cdot (1-0.05)=152>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.05\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.05(1-0.05)}{160}} \\\\  & 0.05\\pm 0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd6bc15-cdc3-53a9-b331-5c5176f9dd4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523414} {:answer/id #uuid "b094ffa0-7616-5773-817f-21c3ca2b8253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.111" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523625} {:answer/id #uuid "ded03ed4-9849-5189-9368-6cbd92efb18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523722} {:answer/id #uuid "ecfecdf1-e7ff-52c1-bbfa-bfad67ae31fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523752} {:answer/id #uuid "f880901b-d44c-5e44-aab2-24fe5ad46d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecfecdf1-e7ff-52c1-bbfa-bfad67ae31fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523752} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7b3069-b6b7-5440-a62a-32c4be352c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0e37f45-88ec-572a-8222-4d40197b3b96" :question/math-academy-id "q-143320" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f0308aa9c39034a938a5f5c63ca049ab21a47fddc20b592d660a70254990ff0.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=75$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=75$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=75\\cdot 0.4=30>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=75\\cdot (1-0.4)=45>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{75}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.146\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e77a14c-9943-5494-ae89-a77c7a1888d0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Residuals From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155561b0-11da-52be-a290-8bc7fd41e681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524422} {:answer/id #uuid "56a2ddd1-cadc-5a93-9a25-8dddff01f271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524532} {:answer/id #uuid "aefdfe8c-fa67-577b-98ee-259e0b602d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524663} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fe8d85-7874-5929-8753-4945ee9ab9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524773} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a545ba-ef49-5db8-a0d9-0458f6933545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0fe8d85-7874-5929-8753-4945ee9ab9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524773} :answer-field/id #uuid "48948107-5211-5ac2-9e2d-522c9771683b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06429645-5834-5aa5-be8c-7530ab34a0e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-134787" :question/problem "![](images/55/553af60aa28cbdd7565259eacc167187bc9c63a5b30cd78a6c14a3c43603fca2.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(5,3).$ So, our actual value is $y=3.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=5$ has the $y$-coordinate $5.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=5.$\n\n![](images/c5/c558c80b7056b794534146d3b99f399c3084806758f78bd6eddb39e8c89248e9.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e77a14c-9943-5494-ae89-a77c7a1888d0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Residuals From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "288479be-98bd-57e4-9608-f6f04980af8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524457} {:answer/id #uuid "a92e5308-f602-5b56-8117-08f3a63517ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524653} {:answer/id #uuid "cc7640de-088c-554a-a442-df97fc11d882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524720} {:answer/id #uuid "e5da0595-ab74-5f54-bd61-a8b4f9a3237a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524785} {:answer/id #uuid "ffffc3bb-9a0e-59c2-9d08-ba493528be35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "288479be-98bd-57e4-9608-f6f04980af8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524457} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15ed523-3e32-50d6-b2d3-15996b2f731b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e01898f3-3b5c-52d9-b3b4-b90e7c403824" :question/math-academy-id "q-134799" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae35183698308f87acb254b7141d025bc55f815c49dea54231c713997c6a22eb.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(10,4).$ So, our actual value is $y=4.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=10$ has the $y$-coordinate $8.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=8.$\n\n![](images/61/61ed1ba4b285ac0d21410ddde91c3ed3882def52cbc500867aa7184114dd8fd8.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162116 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78241be4-1db7-554c-a7ce-7532345f3f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Continuous Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18079953-7a0d-57a4-8843-c654a6bb39fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.891" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526552} {:answer/id #uuid "22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.617" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526579} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8bb5ed-e27a-5e79-a56d-335ca515db9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.581" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526661} {:answer/id #uuid "6f07d653-9ff4-5a87-a55f-d3592fa58cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.714" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526721} {:answer/id #uuid "d50a9899-221a-5f45-912c-5d49f82f44f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.819" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22e80040-d18e-5faa-af39-593a97881279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.617" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526579} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f99177-a32e-55ae-845f-bfa34cf66810" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31869bf1-d8be-5a63-a737-e3c2335ce819" :question/math-academy-id "q-159941" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-8.4+7.4\\text{ }\\,\\theta -6\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-8.4+7.4\\text{ }\\,\\theta -6\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =7.4-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.4-12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 12\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =7.4 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.617\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.617,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162116 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78241be4-1db7-554c-a7ce-7532345f3f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Continuous Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5562092f-3fe0-5bcf-8339-c7daec6026f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.208" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526678} {:answer/id #uuid "56b4bf5e-9806-5a09-a3b3-65589f4e73ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526679} {:answer/id #uuid "8bedb81d-563d-5133-aea2-1add3162bcde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.276" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526783} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8e2520-683e-5f18-9745-b1b59e87e0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526831} {:answer/id #uuid "cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.316" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf461d27-016b-5aa4-a77d-15596b89f756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.316" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526899} :answer-field/id #uuid "34a1e506-0c01-548c-8aeb-dbb4e0856d79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4510ff72-383e-5b2d-aa54-f6d78aa69e2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159939" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent exponential random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\theta }-15.8 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\theta } & =15.8 \\\\ 5 & =15.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.316\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.316,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162116 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78241be4-1db7-554c-a7ce-7532345f3f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Continuous Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23fcd9a5-0cf5-5d2e-97e7-871a82a5410c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.887" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526582} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae6bb66-be05-5dcb-8863-b23c52551053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.788" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526743} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d0c384-6c7e-5b36-a4e1-184863f1c6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.778" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526875} {:answer/id #uuid "f18f76ba-32bc-57a4-ac9a-52ff4682af02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.817" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526977} {:answer/id #uuid "fa0b1bf7-e11d-54c6-a696-04e144a0cba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.878" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ae6bb66-be05-5dcb-8863-b23c52551053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.788" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526743} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e02fd70-4bfa-5ee0-87ea-68ee321a3054" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54d12174-c052-5371-9f75-2200e071a1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-159945" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-9.6+12.6\\text{ }\\,\\theta -8\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-9.6+12.6\\text{ }\\,\\theta -8\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =12.6-16\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.6-16\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 16\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =12.6 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.788\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.788,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162115 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62aa30b-6bf5-5c06-bb1f-8269a6806368" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Discrete Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4162e3d9-b07a-5986-95b7-183bc014b786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526630} {:answer/id #uuid "694a6d77-b1f1-591c-bc99-15a19344806a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526714} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d0294e-3e9f-5fe9-9082-3c690994a0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526909} {:answer/id #uuid "ea506d61-f445-598b-b7b4-71d4e5999d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526969} {:answer/id #uuid "f262d641-ad09-5541-a1b9-b14c2d786ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "694a6d77-b1f1-591c-bc99-15a19344806a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526714} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f74069f-b86f-503f-a061-d9ec00b1c8bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb508aa-a9ee-56b2-be2a-f7d71a96d61c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159963" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ),\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\theta }+\\frac{12}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\theta }+\\frac{12}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{\\theta }+\\frac{12}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{8(\\theta -1)+12\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 8(\\theta -1)+12\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 20\\theta -8 & =0 \\\\ 20\\theta  & =8 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{2}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162115 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62aa30b-6bf5-5c06-bb1f-8269a6806368" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Discrete Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ec69c2-402d-585f-8677-fa0507e34591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526514} {:answer/id #uuid "4f44da59-7cd2-5b7d-af6d-baec2d76fd01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526664} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9f706c-b703-5f30-8657-51f905019ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526757} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cad329-3f44-5a46-8740-0f8924a257f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526998} {:answer/id #uuid "fe90cb0a-c53d-50aa-be27-b8f0505c19f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f44da59-7cd2-5b7d-af6d-baec2d76fd01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526664} :answer-field/id #uuid "332d2628-c9ca-5acc-836b-f4adcf66f467" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "953449db-a540-520d-adff-108b01b9d257" :question/math-academy-id "q-159970" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Poisson random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24) \\\\  & =-4+\\frac{8}{\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+\\frac{8}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-4\\theta +8}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ -4\\theta +8 & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162115 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62aa30b-6bf5-5c06-bb1f-8269a6806368" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Discrete Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a118ec-41f7-5413-a4c8-32753ff0ebca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526635} {:answer/id #uuid "7399eea1-69ca-595e-a652-c19bcbe640a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526728} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb1b0f3-0b13-5864-83a1-105f6280eef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526745} {:answer/id #uuid "7f993abd-7f38-5e5f-8e1e-36f70f05aa31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526756} {:answer/id #uuid "dff4b107-b57d-540e-93c5-bfef91bdbf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44a118ec-41f7-5413-a4c8-32753ff0ebca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526635} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb3d4ca8-f088-5bd5-860c-f0291af054cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a6d182d3-6707-59a8-922c-7df2bb097cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-159971" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Poisson random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-5\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-5\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =-5+\\frac{4}{\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5+\\frac{4}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-5\\theta +4}{\\theta } & =0 \\\\ -5\\theta +4 & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{4}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162115 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a62aa30b-6bf5-5c06-bb1f-8269a6806368" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Discrete Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0450f0af-5d9b-567c-a1ad-d112306dcc16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526511} {:answer/id #uuid "92c2529a-c481-5299-855e-40b34693e071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526790} {:answer/id #uuid "9b090b25-4146-55a8-a5b6-04376c6065c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526804} {:answer/id #uuid "c44e79cb-7368-5b47-96a3-d466cfa63dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526877} {:answer/id #uuid "fd968792-04b8-50fc-800f-35d02d77d269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92c2529a-c481-5299-855e-40b34693e071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526790} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cbe870a-0100-5aae-b454-49e3eb8844b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3abf1cc-eadf-582f-929d-006fffbd1ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159965" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent Bernoulli random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ),\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample?" :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{2}{1-\\theta }\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{2}{\\theta -1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\theta }+\\frac{2}{\\theta -1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{6(\\theta -1)+2\\theta }{\\theta (\\theta -1)} & =0 \\\\ 6(\\theta -1)+2\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 8\\theta -6 & =0 \\\\ 8\\theta  & =6 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}=\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162116 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78241be4-1db7-554c-a7ce-7532345f3f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Continuous Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b7e61a-87f8-5dfb-b095-ddd7b27158f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.765" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526503} {:answer/id #uuid "3de46f7c-9501-5c63-9068-1ec131a5e103" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.734" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526621} {:answer/id #uuid "67ea16bb-5fc8-5966-91a6-89f6143bb573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.617" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526710} {:answer/id #uuid "da20a45a-b8d5-564b-b608-5e3791c34ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.458" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526923} {:answer/id #uuid "f91e9e3e-0341-5556-a0b5-5691bcc58340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.513" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f91e9e3e-0341-5556-a0b5-5691bcc58340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.513" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526996} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0558f9a-6e48-5095-9d94-0109cf7402ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d69af2de-f48e-5047-98b1-6977b8b554d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-159940" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent exponential random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -7.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -7.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\theta }-7.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\theta }-7.8 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\theta } & =7.8 \\\\ 4 & =7.8\\text{ }\\,\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.513\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.513,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162116 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "78241be4-1db7-554c-a7ce-7532345f3f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Maximum Likelihood Estimators for Continuous Probability Distributions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1127dce4-9c1c-5edb-917f-38707912afec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.073" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526536} {:answer/id #uuid "12790bd2-fdb6-5ae2-9339-61f0fb556461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.367" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526538} {:answer/id #uuid "56d327c3-e05f-5754-88f5-dbc857c6043f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526681} {:answer/id #uuid "c15cc0bb-88d7-5a65-b0be-5fe61a791505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.065" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526871} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b77137-f026-54f7-b0e8-585ac5b704af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12790bd2-fdb6-5ae2-9339-61f0fb556461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.367" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526538} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1816a3-cd64-5456-9517-95ae4181b629" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d834043c-817d-50e2-98ca-784bf169d161" :question/math-academy-id "q-159942" :question/problem "Given that the log-likelihood function for a particular sample of independent normal random variables is\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-3.54+2.2\\text{ }\\,\\theta -3\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the maximum likelihood estimate $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}$ from this sample? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "In order to find the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta ,$ we calculate the derivative of the log-likelihood function, set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $\\theta .$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative of $l(\\theta ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}l}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\pi )-3.54+2.2\\text{ }\\,\\theta -3\\text{ }\\,{\\theta }^{2}) \\\\  & =2.2-6\\text{ }\\,\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we set the derivative equal to $0$ and solve for $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.2-6\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 6\\text{ }\\,\\theta  & =2.2 \\\\ \\theta  & ≈0.367\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for this sample, the maximum likelihood estimate of $\\theta $ is $\\overset{^}{\\,\\theta \\,}≈0.367,$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24771a14-af10-5bc8-94d4-17c28b180c84" :knowledge-point/title "Writing and Reading Formal Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cdf484b-66eb-5a9f-96fb-2be6f97d6737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{n}={max}_{n\\in \\{3,5,7,9\\}}c(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533170} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebe1402-cc23-504b-bb1f-600375c70556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{n}={min}_{n\\in \\{3,5,7,9\\}}c(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533212} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf15706-6c17-5503-a77a-8bf5a0786d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{n}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{n\\in \\{3,5,7,9\\}}c(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533397} {:answer/id #uuid "711ef5c5-455c-5d83-901c-33b80c3e7152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{n}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{n\\in c(n)}\\{3,5,7,9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533407} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed4b9d4-3874-51d6-9f79-7bcc2fe66aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{n}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{n\\in \\{3,5,7,9\\}}c(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed4b9d4-3874-51d6-9f79-7bcc2fe66aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{n}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{n\\in \\{3,5,7,9\\}}c(n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533439} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe7e58b2-6cf5-5dc0-a908-c4dfec9a9926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00fdbc42-937e-5a35-be79-3ffa566504b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-355398" :question/problem "Let $c(n)$ be a cost for option $n,$ where $n\\in \\{3,5,7,9\\}.$ We want to write the optimal option $\\overset{^}{n}$ as \"the value of $n$ that makes $c(n)$ as small as possible.\"\n\nWhich of the following correctly defines $\\overset{^}{n}?$" :question/worked-solution "We want $\\overset{^}{n}$ to be an *input value* $n$ where the cost $c(n)$ is as small as possible. \n\nThat is exactly what $\\operatorname{arg}⁡min$ returns.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{n}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{n\\in \\{3,5,7,9\\}}c(n).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24771a14-af10-5bc8-94d4-17c28b180c84" :knowledge-point/title "Writing and Reading Formal Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "450feb6f-7850-5f5d-8795-282d5fc86d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533301} {:answer/id #uuid "76db008e-9696-50d1-b034-aca1854d279f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}={min}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533420} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f54bd2-c0b3-56c8-b6df-6ffa0ee9341a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}={max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533536} {:answer/id #uuid "e29d928e-baa8-5e76-b627-c742ea42fee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{min}_{y\\in w(y)}\\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533701} {:answer/id #uuid "eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eed8db63-444d-5be5-abc8-22b87e87b1b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533725} :answer-field/id #uuid "e07302db-2571-50ff-8138-960f3c5d371d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e94aa36-f8ac-5d68-8a85-88c135136bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-355401" :question/problem "Let $w(y)$ be a weight for node $y,$ where $y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}.$ We want to write the selected node $\\overset{^}{y}$ as \"the value of $y$ that makes $w(y)$ as large as possible.\"\n\nWhich of the following correctly defines $\\overset{^}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "We want $\\overset{^}{y}$ to be an *input value* $y$ where the weight $w(y)$ is as large as possible. \n\nThat is exactly what $\\operatorname{arg}⁡max$ returns.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231452 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24771a14-af10-5bc8-94d4-17c28b180c84" :knowledge-point/title "Writing and Reading Formal Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3613e1aa-20ff-553e-86bf-6acd4c4e754b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}={max}_{y\\in \\{3,7,11,15\\}}g(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533265} {:answer/id #uuid "4e419701-4ca0-5634-aac0-e3a2e78a3ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{^}{y}=\\