(in meters) of several oak trees at certain times $x$ (in years) after they were planted. The scatter plot for the data and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.  \n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(6,6.5).$ So, our actual value is $y=6.5.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=6$ has the $y$-coordinate $5.5.$ So, our estimated value is $\\overset{^}{y}=5.5.$\n\n![](images/ee/eedd1cadb2ba49fb95f9e18f9bef365942e53521e6894a270c9a1e1474f3ed71.png)\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =6.5-5.5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $1\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2eac5ebc-2001-5939-bb01-a23db90d16ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8993" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551777} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7e5d5d-eaba-5b52-abb2-c07c5cabfd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8966" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551845} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3f1472-3696-5c97-aba0-3dd00565cf62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9456" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552073} {:answer/id #uuid "bd23aab7-df07-55de-bc6b-69004196684a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9399" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552116} {:answer/id #uuid "f904190a-a9e5-53be-ad83-84813e7c98d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd23aab7-df07-55de-bc6b-69004196684a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9399" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552116} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a3f931d-2db0-5acf-8977-4c0279c7518b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c142fbc-f51c-5174-b26c-467ddea21c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-248223" :question/problem "A newspaper in a town has $100\\,000$ subscribers, and $56\\,000$ of them have a digital subscription. If the newspaper randomly samples $224$ subscribers, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $112$ and $140$ subscribers (inclusive) in the sample have a digital subscription. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0359\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0351\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0344\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0446\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0436\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0427\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0548\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0537\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0526\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9599\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9608\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9616\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9678\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9686\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9693\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9744\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9750\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9756\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=224,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{56\\,000}{100\\,000}=0.56.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=224(0.56)=125.44>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=224(1-0.56)=98.56>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=224$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.56,\\frac{0.56(1-0.56)}{224}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.56,0.0011).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $112$ and $140$ of subscribers in the sample have a digital subscription, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{112}{224}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{140}{224} \\\\ 0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.625.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.625).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.625) & =P(\\frac{0.5-0.56}{\\sqrt{0.0011}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.625-0.56}{\\sqrt{0.0011}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.81\\le Z\\le 1.96) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.96)-P(Z\\le -1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.96)-Φ(-1.81)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.96)=0.9750,\\,Φ(-1.81)=0.0351.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.5\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.625) & =Φ(1.96)-Φ(-1.81) \\\\  & =0.9750-0.0351 \\\\  & =0.9399.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcbec092-32b6-5e0d-be59-3f0ec18b8c6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8e04cb-b825-58db-8ad8-b03e2d067c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(-1.91)-Φ(0.15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551693} {:answer/id #uuid "334e3f84-c380-5cf4-8954-e5d170c7537d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.91)-Φ(1.65)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551786} {:answer/id #uuid "34c55053-9c11-5d1e-8f12-735d70f72013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.07)-Φ(0.35)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551789} {:answer/id #uuid "7a889bb5-4965-5d5d-b3d9-3edc871df271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.35)-Φ(0.12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551955} {:answer/id #uuid "9559b7c5-7596-50f4-809d-c63614192c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.91)-Φ(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9559b7c5-7596-50f4-809d-c63614192c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.91)-Φ(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552025} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb91e50-0e51-565e-98f0-4e7b44b96808" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b794abe-e844-5b01-84a2-a65b6d920bc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-147484" :question/problem "A call center manager knows that $40%$ of the calls are complaints. If the manager randomly samples $80$ calls, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $32$ but fewer than $36$ of them are complaints. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.4.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.4)=32>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.4)=48>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.4,\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.4,0.0030).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $32$ but fewer than $36$ calls are complaints, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{80}<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <\\frac{36}{80} \\\\ 0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.45).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.45) & =P(\\frac{0.4-0.4}{\\sqrt{0.0030}}<Z<\\frac{0.45-0.4}{\\sqrt{0.0030}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<0.91) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.91)-P(Z<0.00) \\\\  & =Φ(0.91)-Φ(0.00)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.45) & =Φ(0.91)-Φ(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e47d81a-96ba-562d-8953-23216385e09c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.85)-Φ(-1.85)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551729} {:answer/id #uuid "56ccccfb-ea6a-5a3b-82bb-275b7a08df9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.96)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551872} {:answer/id #uuid "75b61603-62d8-520b-abdf-6ee921cee91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.96)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551943} {:answer/id #uuid "93e4abf7-ab00-51bb-883e-429ea9708a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552023} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f8810-daf6-5c36-bcdb-37a7726ca2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae1f8810-daf6-5c36-bcdb-37a7726ca2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-Φ(1.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552084} :answer-field/id #uuid "57bcc9c4-cdd4-55e6-a5b0-8d30dd034164" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "690b1c72-fb42-5766-9459-80c219f09698" :question/math-academy-id "q-146954" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=60$ from a population where $30%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $40%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=60,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=60(0.3)=18>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=60(1-0.3)=42>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=60$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41537c58-9c96-5e6c-b0e6-778ebecb212c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Situations When the CLT for Proportions is Appropriate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1202bf1a-f706-5bcf-af3a-f876dc090d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551703} {:answer/id #uuid "1952d9ec-6286-5a4c-9e84-d0c4ccd6aaa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551717} {:answer/id #uuid "442774be-a20d-58a2-b4e2-2c8322da6b46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551830} {:answer/id #uuid "978d6838-a763-599d-98a7-3c0d59e3b265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552032} {:answer/id #uuid "9f095ae4-fbdf-5a24-8f87-ad73d95fa0d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1952d9ec-6286-5a4c-9e84-d0c4ccd6aaa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551717} :answer-field/id #uuid "c524fbfd-0dc2-5174-8d0f-5797b1138e36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3d4715f-79a5-5718-adbc-5959190456a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-146831" :question/problem "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ students from a class, where $60%$ of the students in the school have an IQ greater than $105.$\n\nII. A sample of $60$ paid TV customers, where $80%$ of the customers watch TV more than $8$ hours per week.\n\nIII. A sample of $40$ players in a team, $90%$ of the players in the league are right-handed.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.6) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60(0.8) \\\\  & =48 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.8) \\\\  & =12 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3fb017-e789-574b-a19f-04df69dcde8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 9.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554012} {:answer/id #uuid "826dcf8a-f14a-5879-aae7-fcfae47a33b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 8.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554130} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3c29cb-bcfa-5582-b197-0fb22fc641ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 9.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554172} {:answer/id #uuid "bf205baf-60e7-57ea-b105-ae0c35dd73ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 10.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554286} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5fe5a5-ce63-5f4a-900e-5dfd444db1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 10.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca5fe5a5-ce63-5f4a-900e-5dfd444db1db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.7\\pm 10.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554314} :answer-field/id #uuid "84de798d-98d3-5bb6-abaa-5ce3efd4cee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14b7fe25-0e56-53c7-82f0-cba7c7a1a3e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-159581" :question/problem "| **Number of hours ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grade ($y$)** | $75$ | $82$ | $90$ | $95$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours four students spent studying for a math exam and the grades they obtained. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.9,$ find a $98%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=61.7+6.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=61.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $98%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.98=0.02\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>6.965)=0.01.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.01}=6.965.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-3.5)^{2}+(3-3.5)^{2}+(4-3.5)^{2}+(5-3.5)^{2} \\\\  & =2.25+0.25+0.25+2.25 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{0.9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{5})}≈10.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=61.7\\pm 10.9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b96016e-6c70-5725-be4a-7c141b414f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553977} {:answer/id #uuid "402a3262-d142-567d-bf7d-c8b89a4726dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553985} {:answer/id #uuid "83ff5ce8-83cc-52e2-94e9-f36326bca2f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554137} {:answer/id #uuid "9870a91b-b9ca-50af-8384-c34539b8a4b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554204} {:answer/id #uuid "f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f661769d-8896-57ab-83d7-39025d585319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.6\\pm 1.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554403} :answer-field/id #uuid "c654e4fd-8bc7-5442-8ca4-e6531d76e850" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24ed500c-c9f9-51ed-ab79-e8e54e985d91" :question/math-academy-id "q-159566" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{18},{y}_{18})$ of $n=18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ the mean square error of the model is $2.1,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=85,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.921)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.6-3.9x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=12.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.921)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{16,0.005}=2.921.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.921\\cdot \\sqrt{2.1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{85})}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=12.6\\pm 1.9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52af52d6-c43f-5772-9249-56cb2e062ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554029} {:answer/id #uuid "804a8e29-21c2-50e1-85dd-8bf5ea96d9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554126} {:answer/id #uuid "8182d4e1-5154-5473-a21d-13951e34b26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554129} {:answer/id #uuid "923c151e-4c0c-5138-872f-79d725e9b5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554191} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7cbdf0-9605-52c2-aebb-1c3a606aa1d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52af52d6-c43f-5772-9249-56cb2e062ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.8\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554029} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dbbd93d-093b-57e4-bfc1-ad59a17bdad5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42368253-8090-5197-973b-1010d704c08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159367" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $11.5,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=143,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.812)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.4+1.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=1.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.812)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.05}=1.812.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.812\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{11.5}{143}}≈0.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.8\\pm 0.5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6ca714-23c9-5847-a9ba-02da4f1cff76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553950} {:answer/id #uuid "44edb47e-bcc6-56b2-a80f-28051a0a9b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553996} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb3fbf6-a548-535d-b241-e4dfd3a43153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554048} {:answer/id #uuid "bd62c1f0-f5e8-5348-b782-6e34e684508d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554280} {:answer/id #uuid "e596f17d-fc27-5a9d-baa8-083f85f564e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c6ca714-23c9-5847-a9ba-02da4f1cff76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.6\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553950} :answer-field/id #uuid "f561bd09-f9f3-5087-9384-dc94b699a804" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b0fde5c-0530-5337-9e62-63e3ac799868" :question/math-academy-id "q-159393" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1-2.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{11},{y}_{11})$ of $n=11$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $0.78,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=110,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.262)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $9$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1-2.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=1-2.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-2.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.262)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{9,0.025}=2.262.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.262\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.78}{110}}≈0.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-2.6\\pm 0.2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62649176-19b3-5dc9-882a-69ef22eb63b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554061} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb2e354-67fd-5920-b3f1-8def0deb29e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554095} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec24db7-f765-5318-ae89-7d5a90748c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554124} {:answer/id #uuid "94f6c867-15bb-5d02-aa0b-4ba6976af87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554198} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c741e7-dfd7-5769-bd67-c9d073965e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94f6c867-15bb-5d02-aa0b-4ba6976af87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\pm 1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554198} :answer-field/id #uuid "67039e40-5de3-570b-bbed-0d3b0e5de178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f56dfe4-956b-5759-bcb7-ccce82fe1dbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159556" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$34$** | **$36$** | **$40$** | **$42$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Hours of sleep ($y$)** | $6.5$ | $7$ | $7.5$ | $8$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages, in years, and the number of hours each of four randomly selected adults slept last night. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=0.6+0.2x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=38,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.0125,$ find an $80%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.886)=0.1,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=0.6+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=0.6+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $80%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.8=0.2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.886)=0.1.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.1}=1.886.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(34-38)^{2}+(36-38)^{2}+(40-38)^{2}+(42-38)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=1.886\\cdot \\sqrt{0.0125\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{38}^{2}}{40})}≈1.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=0.6\\pm 1.3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0286d36b-217f-52e4-8580-e031b6dea31e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residuals Using the Regression Line Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "860cc4ac-4d0f-5373-9938-2c6e8931d240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554146} {:answer/id #uuid "874e5c1d-7313-523e-b2bd-2d3c8fa83a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554151} {:answer/id #uuid "986a6fb3-cec8-5e57-9bf2-aab9895fd98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554203} {:answer/id #uuid "a8513cdf-2331-554c-8660-9e7e500c6f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554242} {:answer/id #uuid "aae6a24f-103b-534b-852d-7d2b3995e87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8513cdf-2331-554c-8660-9e7e500c6f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554242} :answer-field/id #uuid "177b610f-1786-5112-9662-337212616622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66a5e8a2-f155-513a-b41f-dffb1f1fb098" :question/math-academy-id "q-134736" :question/problem "A veterinarian collects data on a puppy's height $y$ (in inches) at certain times $x$ (in months) since it was born. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=2.2x+3.5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the residual after $5$ months if the actual height of the $5$-month old puppy is $15.5\\,in?$" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the estimated height using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =2.2x+3.5 \\\\  & =2.2(5)+3.5 \\\\  & =14.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =15.5-14.5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $1\\,in.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15060daf-a4bc-5eb8-b79b-034a85e0bbf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 18.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553910} {:answer/id #uuid "71ae2911-c3b0-54dd-b735-0b92451fc7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 9.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554099} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f1300c-5d71-5a55-8165-193b27ab2363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 17.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554302} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f2c162-946e-59cc-8842-379aa299e7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 16.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554367} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ebf936-7757-56b0-a68f-58f3fcbd404b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 10.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15060daf-a4bc-5eb8-b79b-034a85e0bbf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 18.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553910} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3ed31d-762f-57be-a238-32e2c1426527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159557" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$67$** | **$70$** | **$73$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe size ($y$)** | $8.5$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and corresponding shoe sizes of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=71.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.338,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.  \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.920)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-26.6+0.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-26.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{0.338\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{71.5}^{2}}{45})}≈18.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-26.6\\pm 18.1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "337b83df-33f6-5e1a-bb43-103241a1e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553960} {:answer/id #uuid "812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554128} {:answer/id #uuid "95ee6076-b2f9-5bbc-b329-206a5101eee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554200} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a799fa-6ed7-5277-8e8a-013cdcf480ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\pm 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554223} {:answer/id #uuid "c235c63d-67de-5b7d-ba35-67ffec0716b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.2\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "812e97bc-01ca-5b39-9e67-dd0fed627e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554128} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0c08eb5-9c27-5ce5-b185-ad184aa151bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "764e52d5-673e-5136-a2ff-4fbb20c5aad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159454" :question/problem "| **Age ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Price ($y$)** | $10.2$ | $9$ | $8.1$ | $5$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages, in years, and corresponding selling prices, in thousands of dollars, of four car models as advertised in a newspaper classifieds last month. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.7-x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=4.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.456,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.7-x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.7-x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-4.5)^{2}+(4-4.5)^{2}+(5-4.5)^{2}+(7-4.5)^{2} \\\\  & =6.25+0.25+0.25+6.25 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.456}{13}}≈0.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-1\\pm 0.5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554591 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "489fcd75-9368-5e9b-acbf-94835400f3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554008} {:answer/id #uuid "92b0899f-5335-5dd0-a82e-12c93b11cd39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554194} {:answer/id #uuid "93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554197} {:answer/id #uuid "b312c9b8-2b8a-552e-98f0-214625871779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554265} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c71797-2619-523f-998b-4dc9f5e6a269" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ebfe14-4c6e-5be6-8ef7-fc1066aec02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.4\\pm 0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554197} :answer-field/id #uuid "b86a25e5-3ce1-5477-9eb2-c277e7527b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159561" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=1.2,$ the mean square error of the model is $0.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=18.9,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.228)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.4+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.4.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.228)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.025}=2.228.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.228\\cdot \\sqrt{0.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18.9})}≈0.7\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.4\\pm 0.7\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ea48dc-2bbd-5e4f-9e61-02d95414ac74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553918} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553983} {:answer/id #uuid "60c1e936-44cd-56c8-8e97-44a0822f3d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 3.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554057} {:answer/id #uuid "ce12ef9b-5ac2-5639-b917-770b6e5414d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554319} {:answer/id #uuid "dd53447b-e183-5dfb-8fe3-9f2284148e07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f4f31a4-eeac-52ca-b025-6e0b059772dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pm 2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553983} :answer-field/id #uuid "caf088fc-e014-5a09-8ceb-3ba512fc68e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "851fa16f-b92a-5672-9bbf-979f258829aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-159560" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=5+0.4x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=4.3,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=330,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=5+0.4x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=5+0.4x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{4.3}^{2}}{330})}≈2.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=5\\pm 2.5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e77a14c-9943-5494-ae89-a77c7a1888d0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Residuals From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27cb4f4c-cab2-57f5-8d0f-1d5d3acbd3da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553945} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd2fbc5-af88-5e57-848a-2616a89a8b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554081} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c198d5-43fb-50f1-82b6-00fe92910869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554227} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ec9563-a8af-54a5-8104-e5811d0f0b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554378} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1533e7-cc35-5e5f-8c0a-d26f3f69e1df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9ec9563-a8af-54a5-8104-e5811d0f0b35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554378} :answer-field/id #uuid "98f4db4e-2c19-5da3-9efd-62ed734fe2e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "855a07b7-e3c5-5f4d-b62e-d65fbbb7b6dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-134788" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f940a05c937b3158ce737959dee4685d216e6d55d6651672aa910cdb2c492f1e.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(5,1).$ So, our actual value is $y=1.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=5$ has the $y$-coordinate $0.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=0.$\n\n![](images/93/937c0307a74881c87e6a33c47b32a1ac2d5124326b13538455640cd9fc88c14e.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2629af92-040b-52b8-9b80-03dbd9c188f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 44.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553938} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554052} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6a69c6-a56f-5da5-a2f4-cda015f6c0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554167} {:answer/id #uuid "91bc6383-5d46-59ca-b246-89d30592f06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 41.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554187} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d3d801-fda0-5c5b-82eb-e97ceb523307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 42.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d9eea9f-4d37-543c-9eaa-babf46254a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-165\\pm 43.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554052} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-159590" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$64$** | **$68$** | **$72$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight ($y$)** | $130$ | $148$ | $164$ | $186$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and the weights, in pounds, of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-165+4.6x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=70,$ and $\\text{MSE}=3.6,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.920)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-165+4.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-165+4.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-165.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(64-70)^{2}+(68-70)^{2}+(72-70)^{2}+(76-70)^{2} \\\\  & =36+4+4+36 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{70}^{2}}{80})}≈43.4\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-165\\pm 43.4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a86111c-7d04-5aa8-9f71-1296e12f0b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553889} {:answer/id #uuid "4d17947d-e25a-5ba5-90a6-ea44648fe558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554019} {:answer/id #uuid "770545bb-976b-5b91-a99e-1a334310037a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554109} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554228} {:answer/id #uuid "d95d02e9-5565-5b9e-8b79-42153a723931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2a2715c-198e-5860-9080-d610e3c815c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7\\pm 1.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554228} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce3aef4c-bd71-5e3d-813c-31f1dae5f512" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "939b2026-f5f3-5f3b-a15d-5ba46809dfda" :question/math-academy-id "q-159541" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$60$** | **$62$** | **$66$** | **$68$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ  ($y$)** | $110$ | $106$ | $108$ | $102$ |\n\nThe table above gives the heights, in inches, and the corresponding scores on an IQ test of four high school students. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=64,$ and $\\text{MSE}=7.7,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>4.303)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=151.3-0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>4.303)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.025}=4.303.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(60-64)^{2}+(62-64)^{2}+(66-64)^{2}+(68-64)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=4.303\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{7.7}{40}}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.7\\pm 1.9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278a0133-b0b4-5d1a-81b8-49903ddff8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553944} {:answer/id #uuid "508865b1-77ef-551a-a36f-b6776f0a785e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554025} {:answer/id #uuid "5db73e1c-4ac3-5171-b6a5-b6b7072792bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554053} {:answer/id #uuid "80cf0bd5-fc8a-5eed-99c9-1117dae18dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554127} {:answer/id #uuid "c4b5686d-bff3-5555-ae5d-0690a2f7e5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4b5686d-bff3-5555-ae5d-0690a2f7e5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554298} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cfd90c1-e81f-5461-89ae-4d8978dbf8e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a0fa4ee-d860-5a97-91a9-24370dbc9085" :question/math-academy-id "q-159376" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $0.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=18.9,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.228)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.4+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=0.2.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.228)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.025}=2.228.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.228\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7}{18.9}}≈0.4\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=0.2\\pm 0.4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0286d36b-217f-52e4-8580-e031b6dea31e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residuals Using the Regression Line Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0690fc09-2812-5def-a5c0-242e3c651d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553882} {:answer/id #uuid "2b24c861-005d-5443-874f-c3222611aae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553948} {:answer/id #uuid "8468c7dc-8ac8-581c-b848-951838cc7b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554142} {:answer/id #uuid "a101f9ee-8feb-534f-9b74-3c43f81b6add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554224} {:answer/id #uuid "c2705e8b-ac1b-58f1-ae18-4b2bb36c099e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a101f9ee-8feb-534f-9b74-3c43f81b6add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554224} :answer-field/id #uuid "76ab6950-70a8-5dae-93a2-6d63d16060f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d55db4e-8bf0-577f-a32c-7bdda150fe93" :question/math-academy-id "q-134719" :question/problem "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=-1.5x+2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the model, what is the residual at $x=12$ if the actual value of $y$ corresponding to this value of $x$ is $-14?$" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =-1.5x+2 \\\\  & =-1.5(12)+2 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =-14-(-16) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bbee7f0-8803-54e8-874c-e570323ea33a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b209ccf-fac3-51cd-a24e-17d94342e720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 183.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554015} {:answer/id #uuid "7e50f150-1d03-5cdd-b228-0b83996b3433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 193.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554121} {:answer/id #uuid "9f303830-c78a-5b75-886c-4c70713deba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 195.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554221} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8ab9d8-4875-5fd3-8eff-2b8511dc815e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 175.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554254} {:answer/id #uuid "e489115e-e8c1-5d14-a654-2c4be9ba3e26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 173.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f303830-c78a-5b75-886c-4c70713deba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "151.3\\pm 195.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554221} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2fd0291-b761-5650-8e97-a72f4f5d21b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a77fcdca-ce6d-5371-a7a2-e886bf8d2462" :question/math-academy-id "q-159596" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$60$** | **$62$** | **$66$** | **$68$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ  ($y$)** | $110$ | $106$ | $108$ | $102$ |\n\nThe table above gives the heights, in inches, and the corresponding scores on an IQ test of four high school students. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=64,$ and $\\text{MSE}=7.7,$ find a $98%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=151.3-0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=151.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $98%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.98=0.02\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>6.965)=0.01.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.01}=6.965.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(60-64)^{2}+(62-64)^{2}+(66-64)^{2}+(68-64)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{7.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{64}^{2}}{40})}≈195.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=151.3\\pm 195.8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172120 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184883f1-85ea-5bd1-8ccd-88226f105da1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5b1a94-4fcd-578f-9a4a-88a6dfb266da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-26.6\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554021} {:answer/id #uuid "82b33e0f-3da2-5ac3-84ca-7016f07baa0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554131} {:answer/id #uuid "8eac7c38-eeac-5c2f-b55d-b4f3d6f40198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554174} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554210} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cd1b87-8a86-58a2-85a0-b0c99abe0e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bd9b13d-b9d6-5c94-bbd9-97339852c840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554210} :answer-field/id #uuid "086dbd14-306d-5d79-b710-e38344655d09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2bc734f-9a4b-543f-ac01-84af782ea390" :question/math-academy-id "q-159518" :question/problem "| **Height ($x$)** | **$67$** | **$70$** | **$73$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe size ($y$)** | $8.5$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and corresponding shoe sizes of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=71.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.338,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.  \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-26.6+0.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=0.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.338}{45}}≈0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=0.5\\pm 0.3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35ca07cb-5b22-501c-89bb-8a7db510076b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553966} {:answer/id #uuid "57ca5bd7-3818-541b-9864-07b885eecc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554041} {:answer/id #uuid "88714f63-0f79-5490-96fc-316d04ce4e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554152} {:answer/id #uuid "c637d326-e1bb-5f3b-830f-2255667712ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554303} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a2f733-69ad-5912-bdd1-d5b4f9a00a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35ca07cb-5b22-501c-89bb-8a7db510076b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553966} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff946275-1749-5fd4-bd3f-74106924808a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c51957e8-149e-57cd-8ee2-fb199eb311cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-159379" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1.5+3.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{18},{y}_{18})$ of $n=18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $2.53,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=484,$ find a $90%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.746)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1.5+3.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=1.5+3.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=3.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.746)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{16,0.05}=1.746.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.746\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2.53}{484}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=3.8\\pm 0.1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0286d36b-217f-52e4-8580-e031b6dea31e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residuals Using the Regression Line Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03780f09-8ea3-5a8a-96e2-a084196fcb98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553874} {:answer/id #uuid "428d4fab-d069-5a00-988d-33cdfa0aae67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553991} {:answer/id #uuid "452ae623-e358-52d4-bb76-b1a2bfd57857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553997} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2a5595-366e-5bca-b60a-4192671812d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554165} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9ad99f-0561-5517-bfcc-0e3a33f16756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "452ae623-e358-52d4-bb76-b1a2bfd57857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553997} :answer-field/id #uuid "790fc10f-cefb-5f65-a677-549fa2e80b47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8526d11-e962-5974-8b9d-23623deb26c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-134753" :question/problem "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=1.8x+1.4.\n$$\n\nAccording to the model, what is the residual at $x=7$ if the actual value of $y$ corresponding to this value of $x$ is $12?$" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =1.8x+1.4 \\\\  & =1.8(7)+1.4 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =12-14 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7813df9e-9641-58a3-8537-e672e5fd079c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Intercept Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f6ae7e-79db-5a6a-9ada-f7fe426e2d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553937} {:answer/id #uuid "460c9059-6d0b-5494-9c9f-84881ad1f6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554000} {:answer/id #uuid "868d4347-c27c-5b05-9b96-6bd24973a73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554149} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fe6c79-a902-5fad-8af9-d0bbdf9b981a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554268} {:answer/id #uuid "e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0159635-2a47-5355-a897-0b6a974a1d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\pm 2.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554355} :answer-field/id #uuid "08326dd0-31c7-5254-9b62-aa5cdeae6bc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159563" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2.7,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.6,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=82.5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.8-3.3x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{2.7}^{2}}{82.5})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.8\\pm 2.8\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554639 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a42fd5c8-9721-5bf4-a1d7-b4b752b09708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals for Slope Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00f51304-e3b6-5932-ad51-bd19b9045d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553867} {:answer/id #uuid "0a29929b-8aeb-5e21-9031-f1ccdab60a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553887} {:answer/id #uuid "49fa42a9-46e5-5910-9c55-1b9972e31902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 1.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554011} {:answer/id #uuid "d7bc35a1-e944-560a-a330-d31779067a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554340} {:answer/id #uuid "db665bb1-2442-5540-9fe3-cca6ff6884bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00f51304-e3b6-5932-ad51-bd19b9045d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6\\pm 0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553867} :answer-field/id #uuid "04602db9-708b-5fcf-bfdb-6de19a2849cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f28f1c0e-4ffd-5dee-98b7-1c475d5129ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-159398" :question/problem "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{9},{y}_{9})$ of $n=9$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.16,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=240,$ find a $90%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.895)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $7$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" :question/worked-solution "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overbrace{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}^{\\text{margin of error}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.4-0.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.895)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{7,0.05}=1.895.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.895\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1.16}{240}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.6\\pm 0.1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176034 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0286d36b-217f-52e4-8580-e031b6dea31e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Residuals Using the Regression Line Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d78fe95-a05e-5eb4-b5c6-d25059010a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553898} {:answer/id #uuid "5f544aa2-e646-591f-8a84-14be73528184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554055} {:answer/id #uuid "91fe5890-b2fc-5848-beaf-a30d780e51eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554189} {:answer/id #uuid "a1810fff-2ec2-5b6f-b1e9-1a2f8c7a4adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554225} {:answer/id #uuid "fc739e9c-e7c6-5a19-823f-c35c9f284575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1810fff-2ec2-5b6f-b1e9-1a2f8c7a4adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554225} :answer-field/id #uuid "29475db4-a660-51b2-8f9a-a2d8b85e63b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f64a4538-a38b-5427-b753-a3e4f5424a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-134737" :question/problem "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=0.75x+2.25.\n$$\n\nAccording to the model, what is the residual at $x=8$ if the actual value of $y$ corresponding to this value of $x$ is $11.25?$" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =0.75x+2.25 \\\\  & =0.75(8)+2.25 \\\\  & =8.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =11.25-8.25 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176033 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e77a14c-9943-5494-ae89-a77c7a1888d0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Residuals From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d738011-9f4c-5d4e-a309-ef64a768c823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554051} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b7e43c-40c5-5e6a-932e-71b4efd2aa35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554247} {:answer/id #uuid "d48ea0cd-c010-5dea-b3a7-cfb311eab498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554330} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b52400-e104-580d-9769-33accdba452b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554362} {:answer/id #uuid "eb398d66-b3c7-5535-b999-ccfa4a862a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d48ea0cd-c010-5dea-b3a7-cfb311eab498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554330} :answer-field/id #uuid "2802197e-59f8-5a42-85de-d389505bc4f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd84baa2-dbd3-5523-aca7-04ab311e0400" :question/math-academy-id "q-134797" :question/problem "![](images/81/815ba0a13bc8cbd7adcfadc63b80d6f2da36c9755166694915fa2f297be27721.png)\n\nAn employee of a car dealership collects data on the value $y$ (in thousands of dollars) of a particular car model $x$ years after its release to the market. The scatter plot for the data and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" :question/worked-solution "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(6,12).$ So, our actual value is $y=12.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=6$ has the $y$-coordinate $10.$ So, our estimated value is $\\overset{^}{y}=10.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab5e194ac03482b1cee6bdb7aa35e323b40a1ad63769e60acbb000adb7aa1e82.png)\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =12-10 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $$2\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "164226e5-15d4-5658-89e3-bcd50cd4659c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.38,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555043} {:answer/id #uuid "68be19d6-216b-577d-b2f8-7073e6ae0ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.38,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.006" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555237} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa95346-6c40-5885-9b17-5047d823cbf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.62,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555295} {:answer/id #uuid "9b61f479-1547-598f-8162-e8595d51c610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.62,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.011" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555375} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbebd0d-2330-54dc-9da4-4269e0fa7655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.38,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfbebd0d-2330-54dc-9da4-4269e0fa7655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.38,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=0.008" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555460} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9340764-7d20-536c-8af6-aa8f9dc6fc8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a90f2432-beab-5d93-b930-00f3398843bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-146445" :question/problem "Suppose that $38%$ of a city's adult population like romance movies. A random sample of $n=30$ adults is taken from the population and asked about their favorite movie genre.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\overset{^}{\\,p},$ the proportion of people in the sample who like romance movies? Round your answers to $3$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic}, \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=38%=0.38,$ and $n=30.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =38%=0.38, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.38(1-0.38)}{30} \\\\  & ≈0.008\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}]}
